{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Postdoc%20Researcher%200304546.pdf": {
      "title": "Postdoc Researcher/PRRC",
      "description": "Postdoc Researcher/PRRC. Posted:  March 25, 2026 \n \n  \n \nPOSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:       POSTDOC RESEARCHER                  DEPT:  PRRC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME        PART TIME   \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $55,000 - $60,000 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 04/03/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThis position supports federally and state-funded research projects on water treatment and critical minerals \nrecover. The role contributes to the execution of research activities by conducting laboratory experiments, \nanalyzing and interpreting data, and preparing technical reports and peer-reviewed manuscripts. This position \nalso provide mentorship and guidance to graduate students and undergraduate students. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nConducting laboratory experiments  \nPreparing technical reports and peer-reviewed manuscripts  \nMentorship to graduate students \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nPh.D. or other doctorate level equivalent in membranes and membrane-base separations.   Membrane dope \nformulation and preparation. Membranes-based processes performance evaluation. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS:  \n \nMembrane process modeling.  \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  O \n15 - 30 pounds S \n30 - 50 pounds  \n50 - 100 pounds  \n100 + pounds  \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding   40% Sitting  30% Walking 30% Pulling    \nPushing    Lifting     Stooping   Kneeling   \nCrawling    Climbing   Reaching    Other \n \n Apply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Postdoctoral%20Research%20Scientist-job%20advertisement.pdf": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Research Scientist.  \n \nPOSTDOCTORAL RESEARCH SCIENTIST/ POWER AND ENERGY SYSTEMS \nPosition Type: Full Time \nSalary Range: $55,000 - $60,000 \nNew Mexico Tech (NMT) invites applications for a postdoctoral research fellow position in the \nbroad area of power and energy systems. This is a joint position between the Petroleum Recovery \nResearch Center (PRRC) and the Department of Electrical Engineerin g. The initial appointment \nwill be for 12 months and can be extended based on performance. The position presents an \nexcellent career development opportunity with an interdisciplinary team at NMT. The postdoctoral \nresearch fellow is expected to participate in project research activities while interacting with \ngraduate and undergraduate research assistants. There will be peer-reviewed publications, proposal \ndevelopment, and other training opportunities. \n \nRequired Qualifications: \n• Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering, Power Systems, Power Electronics, Energy Systems, or \nother related fields;  \n• Strong research background in power and energy systems, preferably in smart grids, \nmicrogrids, or distributed energy resources;  \n• Familiar with MATLAB, Python, and other programming tools; \n• Excellent writing and oral communication skills. \n \nPreferred Qualifications: \n• Hands-on experience with power system hardware, real -time simulation (OPAL -RT, \nRTDS, Typhoon HIL, etc), hardware-in-the-loop (HIL/PHIL), or laboratory experiments;  \n• Experience with microgrid or smart grid testbeds; \n• Experience integrating distributed energy resources, power electronics, or control systems; \n• Familiarity with communication protocols (IEC 61850, Modbus, DNP3, etc); \n• Experience in grant proposal writing. \n \nPosition open until filled; for status of the position, please send inquiries to Dr. Qingsong Cui at \nqingsong.cui@nmt.edu. To apply, please email your application package in a single PDF \ndocument to nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu and copy to qingsong.cui@nmt.edu and \nsai.wang@nmt.edu, with \" NMT-Postdoc-PES\" in the email subject line. The package should \ncontain a cover letter highlighting expertise and experience most suited to the position, the most \nrecent CV, transcript(s), representative publications, and contact information of 2–3 references. \nNMT is committed to creating a community in which a diverse population can learn, live, and \nwork in an atmosphere of tolerance, civility and respect for the rights and sensibilities of each \nindividual. NMT is an Equal Opportunity Employer. NMT provides exc ellent benefits, including \nhealth, vision, dental, and a generous retirement plan. \n  \n"
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Postdoctoral%20Research%20Scientist%200918326.pdf": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist. Posted:  September 11, 2026 \n \n  \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: POSTDOCTORAL RESEARCH SCIENTIST   DEPT: PRRC \n \nREG     TEMP      FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $50,000 - $60,000  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by New \nMexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 09/21/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY WITHIN \nTHE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThis position will be in the Reservoir Evaluation and Advanced Computational Technologies (REACT) section \nof the PRRC. As a member of this section, the candidate will be part of a an integrated team of geoscientists \nand engineers working on applied research projects in hydrocarbon resources development, geologic carbon \nsequestration, and water resources management. This position will have expertise in site characterization, \ngeological modeling, core analysis petroleum system modeling, well logging and expertise in writing proposals \nand leading research projects. This position will also supervise undergraduate and graduate students. \n \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nStructural Geology  \nGeological Modeling  \nWell Log Interpretation  \nWriting Proposals/ Publication  \nMentoring Students \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nPh.D. or other doctorate level equivalent  in Geology/Geoscience. Proficient in Structural Geology. Efficient \nin developing Geological Modeling. Know ledge in using Petrel, Kingdon.  Knowledge in well log \ninterpretation. \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 60% Sitting 10% Walking 10% Pulling  10% \nPushing   10% Lifting 15% Stooping  10% Kneeling  15% \nCrawling  5% Climbing  5% Reaching 10% Other \n \n  \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Coordinator%20Bureau%20Business%20Office%200919026.pdf": {
      "title": "Coordinator/Bureau Business Office",
      "description": "Coordinator/Bureau Business Office.  Posted:  September 18, 2026 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:  COORDINATOR/BUSINESS OFFICE DEPT:  BUREAU OF GEOLOGY \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $48,672 – $55,973 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 09/29/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe Coordinator, Bureau Business Office provides financial and administrative support for the Bureau of \nGeology and Mineral Resources business office. This position processes Bureau payroll and travel requests \nand reimbursement requests, maintains the Bureau business office invoice system, processes journal \nvouchers (JVs) and payroll journal vouchers (PJVs), prepares payroll distribution forms, and supports \nprocessing of procurement documents in coordination with the Financial Analyst. The Coordinator, Bureau \nBusiness Office reviews departmental transactions for compliance with institutional policies and procedures, \nsupports financial and administrative reporting funct ions, and works closely with internal staff, principal \ninvestigators, lab managers, and the Bureau Associate Director for Finance to ensure accurate financial \nrecords, proper documentation, and adherence to deadlines. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nProcess Bureau payroll and prepare payroll distribution forms  \n \nProcess journal vouchers (JVs) and payroll journal vouchers (PJVs) to correct, transfer, or reclassify financial \ntransactions  \n \nMaintain the Bureau business office invoice system, ensuring timely routing and payment  \n \nReview and process travel requests and reimbursement requests for compliance with travel policy  \n \nReview departmental transactions for compliance with institutional policies and sponsor requirements. \nProvide financial/administrative support for  contract and operational activities. Support financial and \nadministrative reporting functions  \n \nSupport processing of procurement documents in coordination with the Bureau coordinator \n \n \n \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor's Degree in Accounting, Finance, Business Administration, or related field. Plus a concentration in \naccounting (concentration is introduction to accounting plus 12 additional hours in accounting). 6 hours may \nbe waived for experience (1 year experience=3 hours and 3 years experience=6 hours).  Payroll processing \nprocedures. Journal voucher (JV) and payroll journal voucher (PJV) processing. Accounts payable / invoice \nprocessing systems R Institutional financial/accounting software (e.g., Banner). Spreadsheet software (e.g., \nMicrosoft Excel). Travel reimbursem ent policies and procedures.  Strong attention to detai l and accuracy \nunder deadlines. Written and verbal communication skills \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nGrant and contract account monitoring. Procurement processes and documentation. \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  S \n15 - 30 pounds  \n30 - 50 pounds  \n50 - 100 pounds  \n100 + pounds  \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 10% Sitting 80% Walking 5% Pulling  \nPushing  Lifting 5% Stooping  5% Kneeling   \nCrawling    Climbing  Reaching  5% Other \n \n  \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Manager%20Publication%20Sales%20Office%200817726.pdf": {
      "title": "Manager/Publication Sales Office",
      "description": "Manager/Publication Sales Office. Posted: August 26, 2026 \n \nPOSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \nTITLE: MANAGER/PUBLICATION SALES OFFICE       DEPT: BUREAU OF GEOLOGY \nREG 🗹🗹 TEMP ◻ FULL TIME 🗹🗹 PART TIME ◻ \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $54,000 - $65,000 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater. \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and ret irement which is largely paid \nby New Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH:  09/04/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING. APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThis position is responsible for daily operations of the Publication Sales Office at the Bureau. The \nsuccessful applicant will oversee and be an active participant in all functions of this office. Responsibilities \ninclude, but are not limited to marketing; marketing inventory to NMBG community, NMGS community, \nlocal/state community as well as faculty/staff/student community; travel to events, cultivate vendor \nrelationships, publication sales, departmental budgeting and accounting; inventories: curating inventory \nand product development; database management related to publications; internal and external support; \nphone/email/walk-in inquiries (from the public at large and the university community) related to geologic \nresources of the state, and the resources of the Bureau of Geology. This successful applicant will \nsupervise and provide training to the Publication Specialist position as well as student employees in both \nthe Bookstore and Museum. \n \nJOBS FUNCTIONS: \n \nStore operations and compliance: Oversees day to day operations of bookstore, including staffing, \nscheduling, opening/closing, managing cashdrawers, point of sale, bank deposits, daily sales logging  \n \nMarketing, inventory, and merchandising management: Responsible for overall marketing and networking \nefforts, including offsite presence at conferences and meetings, wholesales, advertising, and promotion of \nnew Bureau publications; responsible for development of new products, curating inventory, vendor \nrelationships, visual merchandising, and loss prevention  \n \nSales and financial performance: Monitors KPI’s, tracks budget, oversees and manages the day-to-day \noperating finances of the Bureau’s printing services, which include large-format plotting, digital and specialty \nprinting, binding, and other finishing work.  \n \nTeam leadership and development: Supervise Publications Resource Specialist and student workers, \nresponsible for training, performance evaluation, performance coaching  \n \nResponds to or directs inquiries from the general public regarding geology of New Mexico and the geologic \nresources available through the Bureau and other state and federal agencies. Problem-solving and \ncustomer resolution.  \n \nManages Bureau relationships with the NMGS, including sale and inventory of NMGS publications, and \nmanaging financial agreements between the Bureau and NMGS and NMGS Foundation. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor’s degree in Business or Marketing. Knowledge of Bureau of Geology’s mission and staff specialties. \nExcellent interpersonal skills. Accounting experience. Supervisory experience. Marketing experience. \nDatabase experience.  \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nFamiliarity with publishing and/or publication sales. Printing services experience. Current, valid NM drivers \nlicense. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS: \nStanding   20% Sitting   50% Walking   12% Pulling   2% \nPushing   2% Lifting   6% Stooping   2% Kneeling   2% \nCrawling  Climbing  2% Reaching   2% Other 1% \n \n \n \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Sr.%20Database%20Administrator%20Bureau%200818026.pdf": {
      "title": "Senior Database Administrator",
      "description": "Senior Database Administrator.  Posted:  August 28, 2026 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:  SENIOR DATABASE ADMINISTRATOR  DEPT:  BUREAU OF GEOLOGY \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $70,720 - $77,792 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 09/08/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe New Mexico Bureau of Geology and Mineral Resources (NMBGMR) is seeking a Database \nAdministrator (DBA) to join the Data Services team. The successful candidate will work as part of the Data \nServices team to design, develop, secure, and maintain a variety of local and cloud- based database \nmanagement systems and databases to support research scientists, staff, and a wide range of external \nstakeholders. This position plays a key role in the ongoing efforts to modernize our data infrastructure, \nensuring high-quality data that supports the Bureau’s mission of improving data access, transparency, and \ninteroperability. The DBA will collaborate closely with full -stack developers, product managers, program \nmanagers, IT, end users, and others to ensure that data and database- related services are reliable, \nresponsive, technically sound, and meet the needs of the Bureau and our external partners. On-site, hybrid, \nand remote options are available.   \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nDatabase Development: In collaboration with other Data Services staff, assists with technical/schema design, \nquery development, data pipelines, feature implementation, normalization/optimization, integration, legacy \ndatabase migration, QA/testing. Work closely with all stakeholders to determine requirements and manage \nthe full project lifecycle using common industry methodologies/best practices.  \n \nDatabase Management: Maintain and support databases in Microsoft SQ L Server, MySQL, PostgreSQL, \nCloud SQL; responsible for maintenance, monitoring, replication, performance/availability, compute/storage \ncapacity planning, virtual machine management, backups/recovery, security, and troubleshooting. Maintain a \nworking knowl edge of current best practices and emerging trends in database technologies and \nmanagement.  \n \nWeb & Middleware Development: Depending on skill set and project needs, may assist Data Services full -\nstack web developers with designing complex queries, forms, API’s, and code to facilitate the use of data by \nthe public and stakeholders outside the bureau through the bureau’s website and web services.  \n \nOther duties as assigned, in support of broader NMBGMR and Data Services initiatives \n \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor's Degree  in Computer/Data Science, IT, or a related field. Candidates with highly relevant \nprofessional experience with degrees in other areas will be considered on a case- by-case basis.  Relevant \nprofessional experience in managing enterprise database management  systems Relevant professional \nexperience in database development. Excellent problem-solving, communication, and teamwork skills. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nProficiency in one or more programming languages (e.g., Python, JavaScript/Typescript, C#, or similar). \nExperience deploy ing systems and applications using cloud infrastructure (AWS, Azure, GCP) or \ncontainerization (Docker)/virtual machines. Understanding of software architecture principles, version control \n(Git), and CI/CD workflows. Experience with agile software devel opment practices and collaboration tools \n(e.g., GitHub, Jira, or similar).  Understanding of open data standards and interoperability frameworks.  \nInterest in applying technology to support water management, geoscience, and policy in New Mexico.  \nFamiliarity with geospatial or environmental data systems. Experience with web application/services \ndevelopment and the creation of web infrastructure/middleware to serve geospatial databases online is \ndesired. \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  O \n15 - 30 pounds N \n30 - 50 pounds N \n50 - 100 pounds N \n100 + pounds N \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding  Sitting  Walking  Pulling  \nPushing  Lifting  Stooping   Kneeling   \nCrawling    Climbing  Reaching   Other \n \n  \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Administrative%20Secretary%20I%20EES%20and%20Mechanical.pdf": {
      "title": "Administrative Secretary I/E&ES and Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": "Administrative Secretary I/E&ES and Mechanical Engineering.  Posted:  October 6, 2026 \n \n \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:      ADMINISTRATIVE SECRETARY I                                                    \n \nDEPT:  E&ES AND MECHANICAL ENGINEERING \n \nREG    TEMP      FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE  $15.60 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 10/13/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThis position is a shared position between two departments. Part time in Earth and Environmental \nScience and part time in Mechanical Engineering. \n \nThe Administrative Secretary provides administrative support to an academic department and assists with \nthe efficient coordination of daily operations. Responsibilities include handling routine correspondence, \nscheduling appointments, maintaining accurate records, and responding to inquiries in a timely and \nprofessional manner.  \n \nThis position supports event planning, purchasing activities, report preparation, and other  departmental \nadministrative functions. The Administrative Secretary ensures assigned tasks are completed accurately and \non schedule and may provide guidance and support to entry-level staff or student workers. This role requires \nstrong attention to detail , organizational skills, effective communication, and a commitment to customer \nservice. The position contributes to efficient departmental operations and promotes professional and \nresponsive communication with faculty, staff, students, and other stakeholders. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nProvides clerical and administrative support to department staff, faculty, or supervisors, performing a variety \nof office functions including scheduling, correspondence, filing, data entry, and document preparation under \ngeneral supervision.  \n \nSchedules appointments, coordinates meetings, and manages calendars for assigned supervisors or staff, \npreparing basic agendas, assembling meeting materials, reserving rooms, and distributing meeting notes or \nfollow-up items as directed.  \n \nAssists with departmental purchasing and procurement activities, processing purchase requisitions, obtaining \nquotes from vendors, tracking orders, verifying deliveries, and maintaining records of departmental \nexpenditures in accordance with institutional policies.  \n \nMonitors and reconciles (under appropriate oversight and supervision) departmental budgets and \nexpenditures, travel authorizations, reimbursement claims, and procurement card reconciliations, and \nproviding regular financial summaries to supervisory staff.  \n \nMaintains and organizes departmental files, records, and office supplies, ensuring materials are current, \naccessible, properly labeled, and stored in compliance with institutional records retention and confidentiality \npolicies.  \n \nAssists with planning and coordinating departmental events, workshops, and activities, supporting logistics \nincluding room reservations, supply ordering, signage preparation, setup, and breakdown as directed by \nsupervisory staff.  \n \nAssists faculty and staff with running repor ts, gathering data and student information, and tracking \ninformation with moderate supervision.  \n \nPerforms other related duties as assigned in support of departmental operations, office administration, \ncustomer service functions, or emerging administrative needs. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nHigh School (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, composition and math. Read and comprehend \ninstructions, write information, and complete simple forms. Must have at least three (3) years of prior \nexperience \n \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  S \n15 - 30 pounds  \n30 - 50 pounds  \n50 - 100 pounds  \n100 + pounds  \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding   10% Sitting  10% Walking  5% Pulling    \nPushing      Lifting     5% Stooping   Kneeling   \nCrawling   Climbing  Reaching   Other \n \n  \n Apply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Assistant%20or%20Associate%20Vice%20President%20of%20Academic%20Affairs%201019826.pdf": {
      "title": "Asst. or Assoc. Vice President",
      "description": "Asst. or Assoc. Vice President.  \n \n \n \nAssistant or Associate Vice President for Academic Affairs \n \nNew Mexico Tech seeks applications for either an Assistant or Associate Vice President for Academic \nAffairs with salary and rank commensurate with experience and qualifications. This position reports \ndirectly to the Provost, Vice President for Academic Affairs (VPAA). The Assistant or Associate Vice \nPresident for Academic Affairs (AVPAA) is the internal and external advocate for New Mexico Tech who \n(i) serves as or assists the Accreditation Liaison Officer (ALO) for New Mexico Tech with the Higher \nLearning Commission, (ii) leads preparation of documents needed for reaffirmation of accreditation and \nother reporting (e.g., NC-SARA); (iii) is the campus lead for assessment of student learning, (iv) is the \ncampus lead for faculty development, (v) is a primary point of contact for undergraduate student \nacademic issues, including handling academic honesty cases and student grievances for academic \nmatters; (vi) oversees interdisciplinary programs (e.g., Biotechnology) and activities connecting \nacademic and student affairs (e.g., Library); and (vii) assists the VPAA with personnel matters, policy \nreview and development, and other tasks assigned. This is a non-tenure line, limited term position - a \ntwo-year appointment. \n \nRequired Qualifications: A PhD or equivalent doctorate-level degree in a STEM field is required.  The \napplicant should have demonstrated administrative experience (1) with accreditation, assessment, \nacademic planning, and evaluation of educational effectiveness; (2) in effectively communicating with \nothers that includes faculty, staff, and students; (3) in prioritizing tasks, managing time, and working \nwith minimal supervision; and (4) in handling academic and personnel concerns. Individuals must have a \nminimum of eight years of experience in higher education with at least three years at a leadership level.  \nExperience in STEM teaching and research is preferred.  Anticipated appointment date is January 11, \n2026, although an earlier start-date is negotiable. \n \nInstitution: A public research institution, New Mexico Tech continues to be a top academic rankings as \nNew Mexico's Best College, College with the Best Academics, Best Value College, and New Mexico's Top \nPublic University. \n \nApplication Instruction: Candidates must apply by e-mail, attaching a single PDF document that \nincorporates all of the following: [1] cover letter; [2] curriculum vitae (CV); and [3] names and contact \ninformation for three references familiar with the candidate’s record of administration and academic \nleadership.  Please do not include transcripts, publications, or other materials not requested above.  \nMaterials should be sent to nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu c/o and copied to lique.coolen@nmt.edu, with \n“AVPAA application” in the e-mail subject line.  For additional information, please contact Dr. Lique \nCoolen (email: lique.coolen@nmt.edu).  Applications received submitted by November 14, 2026 will \nreceive full consideration.  \n \n\nEqual Employment Opportunity:  New Mexico Tech is committed to creating a community in which a \ndiverse population can learn, live, and work in an atmosphere of tolerance, civility, and respect for the \nrights and sensibilities of each individual. New Mexico Tech is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All those \ninterested in the position are encouraged to apply, including women, individuals from marginalized \ngroups, and persons with disabilities. \n \nBenefits: NMT is committed to enabling healthy work-life balance for its employees, with excellent \nbenefits (health, vision, dental), tuition fee waiver for self and dependents, a generous retirement plan, and \naccess to a childcare center on campus. \n \nRegional Attractions: New Mexico Tech is located in, Socorro, in the scenic Rio Grande River Valley of \ncentral New Mexico, just one hour south of Albuquerque with its many attractions and convenient \nairport access.  Socorro itself is a place with rich history and tradition, offering easy and low-cost living.  \nNearby mountains and desert canyons provide opportunities for excellent hiking, climbing, and biking, \nand photography. The Bosque del Apache National Wildlife Refuge, located just south of Socorro along a \nmajor north-south flyway, offers some of the best birding in the USA. \n "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Geology%20Field%20Technician%200509726.pdf": {
      "title": "Geology Field Technician",
      "description": "Geology Field Technician.  Posted:  May 18, 2026 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: GEOLOGY FIELD TECHNICIAN  DEPT: E&ES \n \nREG    TEMP      FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $15.00 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 05/29/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nTo provide field assistance for geological fieldwork in southern Wyoming and northern Utah during the \nsummer of 2026.  \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nGeological Field Assistance \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor's Degree in Geology, Geoscience or related field. Training and experience in geological fieldwork. \nExperience conducting geological fieldwork in the western United States. Completion of undergraduate \n\"Geology Field Camp\" or equivalent. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds   \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds  \n50 - 100 pounds  \n100 + pounds  \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 40% Sitting  Walking 60% Pulling  \nPushing  Lifting  Stooping   Kneeling   \nCrawling    Climbing  Reaching   Other \n \n  \n Apply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Postdoctoral%20Reasearcher%20Physics%201217925.pdf": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher/Physics",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher/Physics. Posted:  December 11, 2025 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: POSTDOCTORAL RESEARCHER  DEPT: PHYSICS \n \nREG    LIMITED TERM  TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $50,000 - $60,000 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent * CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe Department of Physics and the Langmuir Laboratory for Atmospheric Research at New Mexico Tech \nare seeking a postdoctoral scholar to perform research on the field of lightning and thunderstorm studies. \nThe specific project is focused on developing new optical instrumentation for lightning research, and to \nperform multi-band observations (optical + radio + X -rays) at the Langmuir Laboratory field facility. At N ew \nMexico Tech, the postdoctoral scholar will be part of a large team working on lightning research consisting \nof professors, emeriti, research scientists, staff, and students. The postdoctoral scholar is expected to \nperform independent research, but at the same time to work well with the team, and to assist with \nsupervising students in the Department of Physics. The scholar shall also have the opportunity to \nchampion a wide suite of instruments (learning to operate, maintain, and apply their data effectively), \nincluding the lightning mapping array, high- speed cameras, photometric arrays, neuromorphic sensors, \nelectric field change antennas, field mills, X-ray sensors, and more. \n \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nTake a leadership role in developing new optical instrumentation which advances the frontier of lightning \nobservation.  \n \nParticipate in Summer observational campaigns at Langmuir Lab. Analyze data.  \n \nReport the results in the format of peer-reviewed journal publications and conference presentations.  \n \nSupport the maintenance of existing instrumentation at Langmuir Lab.  \n \nSupervise students. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nPh.D. or other doctorate level equivalent in Physics, Electrical Engineering, Atmospheric Sciences, or related \nfield.  \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nDemonstrated experience/ability in all three categori es listed above: instrumentation development, field \ncampaigns, and lightning research, plus experience with computer programming. Experience with either \noptical spectroscopy or with operation of neuromorphic cameras. \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 15% Sitting 37% Walking 10% Pulling 5% \nPushing 10% Lifting 5% Stooping  2% Kneeling  2% \nCrawling   3% Climbing 2% Reaching  5% Other  4% \n \n  \nFor best consideration please apply by March 1, 2026 \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Technical%20Services%20Librarian%200407626.pdf": {
      "title": "Technical Services Librarian",
      "description": "Technical Services Librarian. Posted:  April 20, 2026 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: TECHNICAL SERVICES LIBRARIAN   DEPT: LIBRARY \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE 50,000  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 04/29/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe Technical Services Librarian will develop and implement with the Library Director an evaluation of \npolicies, procedures, and training that will support the Library’s mission of improved access to information, \nstudent success, and research support. The Technical Services Librarian will locate and create content to \nsupport academic instruction with the Director and the Public Services Librarian; explore, evaluate, and \nencourage deployment of emergent technologies into library programs and services. Common job \nresponsibilities will include: information management, acquisitions, vendor management, invoice processing, \nmanaging digital assets and subscriptions including support for technical services issues, coordination and \nmanagement of the library’s physical PC system and website management. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nNetwork with NMT Faculty and Administration; create and maintain relationships with Academic Departments \nto insure communication, development and delivery of digital programming. Develop and maintain strong \nproductive relationships with colleagues, students, faculty, staff and NMT administration.   \n \nProcess and Maintain Academic Subscriptions and Data Bases, U pdates holdings, User statistics, \nBibliographic records. Troubleshoots issues within the digital collections.   \n \nWorks in concert with the Operations Coordinator to process and insure timely payment of invoices in \nsupport of the Library Director.   \n \nEngages in Information Literacy Instruction and Systems Instruction with the Public Services Librarian and \nLibrary Director.  \n \nWith the Library Director implements deployment of emergent technologies into library programs and \nservices. Engages with improvement measures for digital collection, including conversations with faculty and \nresearchers on assess and deliverability of information.   \n \n \n \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor's Degree (Any BA or BS degree which supports Library Science activities such as in the \nHumanities, Science or Engineering ). Master's Degree, a n ALA certified Library and Information Science \nMaster’s Degree is required for this position.  Knowledge of Acq uisition Procedures. Competence in ALA/ \nACRL guidelines and foundations for Academic Librarians to support overall mission of the Library and NMT. \nCompetence in in digital asset management including eBook, eJournal, and database management and \ntechnical troubleshooting of said digital assets. Knowledge of OCLC Worldshare, Ex Libris Alma, EBSCOnet, \nor other library ILS systems. \n \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nStrong Interpersonal Skills, employing effective communication, organization, analytic, and problem -solving \nskills. Experience with LaTeX, Creative AI programming. Experience with basic PC and Mac troubleshooting, \nboth software and hardware issues. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds O \n30 - 50 pounds S \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 20% Sitting 40% Walking 24% Pulling 2% \nPushing 2% Lifting 2% Stooping 2% Kneeling 2% \nCrawling 2% Climbing 2% Reaching 2% Other \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Client%20Services%20Sup.%200916524.pdf": {
      "title": "Client Services Supervisor",
      "description": "Client Services Supervisor. Posted:  September 30, 2024 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: CLIENT SERVICES SUPERVISOR   DEPT: ITC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $60,320 - $76,305 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: October 9, 2024* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO \nAPPLY WITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \nInstalls and maintains the campus data network equipment, include Firewalls, IDS/IPS, Traffic/Bandwidth \nShapers, Routers, and various other Layer2/3 devices. In addition will be responsible for support network \napplication servers located at New Mexico Tech, remote sites or in the collocation areas. This support \nincludes specification, design, and implementation of software, hardware, backups, and security.  Designs, \ninstalls, configures, manages, and maintains all types of network attached devices such as printers, \nsecurity devices, IP telephones, control devices, PCs, storage devices, firewalls, intrusion prevention \ndevices, and various types of servers. Designs, installs, configures, manages Wireless Networking \nInfrastructure. This position is responsible for providing support to users on the network for software, \nhardware, security and various network services/devices such as printers, scanners, IP telephones, VPN \nstorage, email, etc. Works with senior level administrator to provide technical support in the areas of \nhardware and software installation and the configuration and maintenance of Windows based systems as \nwell as Unix based systems. Install and manage Unix based and Windows servers. Support a large base \nof diverse client PC configurations, including installation, configuration and user support including user \naccounts. Works with security staff to operate a secure environment. Supervise technical staff. Other \nduties as assigned. \n \nJOBS FUNCTIONS: \n \nConfigure and maintain Network Support Infrastructure. Including, but not limited to: Firewalls, IDS/IPS, \nTraffic/Bandwidth shaping, Wireless, Routers and switches. \nDocumentation and Billing. \nServer update, and management. Including but not limited to: Windows/Linux/other Server, turnkey and \nother integrated devices, work with end stations. \nMonitors network activity to determine and address capacity issues, security threats, and outages. \nWork on independent IT projects. \nInstall and test network device software and hardware, including, but not limited to: IP Phones, Network \nCameras, Security Devices, Sensors, etc. \nSupervise support staff and students. \n \n \n \n \n \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nAssociate's degree or completion of program 18+ months after high school in Information Technology, \nComputer Science, or related discipline and at least two years performing tasks related to the job \nrequirements. Appropriate work experience may be substituted for some educational requirements.  2 \nyears of experience with network hardware and software installation, and problem resolution. Experience \nwith LINUX/UNIX-based clients and servers. Able to obtain and maintain CJIS certification. Be able to \nclimb ladders and access roof top mounted equipment. Valid New Mexico Drivers License. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor's Degree in Information Technology, Computer Science, or related discipline. Appropriate work \nexperience may be substituted for some educational requirements. Experience in the installation & \nmaintenance of Windows 2016 (or later) servers. Knowledge of basic UNIX and Windows \nscripted/automation and utilities. Experience with Windows Clients (Win10, or later) \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 25% Sitting 25% Walking 15% Pulling 5% \nPushing Lifting 5%  Stooping 5% Kneeling \nCrawling Climbing 10% Reaching 10% Other \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Financial%20and%20Data%20Analyst%20r%201019726.pdf": {
      "title": "Financial and Data Analyst",
      "description": "Financial and Data Analyst. Posted:  October 6, 2026 \n \n  \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: FINANCIAL AND DATA ANALYST         DEPT: BUDGET AND ANALYSIS  \n \nREG     TEMP      FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $60,320 - $69,368  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 10/14/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nUnder general direction, provides financial support to the Budget & Analysis office.  Assists with position \ncontrol for entire campus.  Interac ts with different departments and ensures quality control of the financial \nsystem.  This position will assist the finance area with the gathering of data and reporting to outside \nagencies.  Performs a wide variety of analytical duties to include budget rel ated matters and financial \nreporting.    \n \nJOB DUTIES: \n \nResearches, compiles and prepares financial and accounting data for studies and reports in a clear and \nconcise manner.  Prepares tables, slides, graphs, and charts to showcase data and trends clearly.  Performs \naccount reviews and audits  \n \nServes as lead in gathering information and data for various reporting requirements to outside agencies.  \nWorks closely with the Institutional Research office.  \n \nPerforms wide variety of analytical duties to include budget related matters and financial reporting to ensure \nfinancial system accuracy.  Reviews routine monthly financial and budgetary reports for accuracy.  Produces \nBudget Revision and Journal Vouchers as necessary.  \n \nAssists the office with the position control process.  Works with the HR office to clear up discrepancies to \nensure accuracy and equity.  \n \nAssists in the yearly budget cycle of preparing budgets, reporting actuals, advising on revisions, and helping \nwith transparency.  Includes reviewing banner online approvals.  \n \nPerforms special assignments, projects and other duties as assigned. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nBachelor’s degree required in Business, Accounting or other related field. Proficient in Microsoft Office \nproducts and experience with advanced functions including significant expert ise with excel spreadsheets.  \nKnowledge of accounting standards and basic financial operations.  Ability to analyze complex multiple \ntransactions and apply accounting theory, policies and procedures, and regulations.  Ability to maintain \nconfidentiality of records and information. Experience with reading and interpreting fiscal and other ty pes \nof administrative reports.  Ability to interact in a professional, pleasant and enthusiastic manner with the \nuniversity community and the public.Strong critical thinking and problem solving skills.  Teamwork and \ninterpersoal skills.  Strong verbal and written communications skills.  Demonstrated high level of accuracy, \nthoroughness and attention to detail.  Ability to organize wor k effectively, conceptualize and prioritize \nobjectives and exercise independent judgement based on an understanding of organizational policies and \nactivities. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nExperience working with Banner or similar ERPs and Argos or similar rep orting software. Fund accounting \nand budget experience in higher education. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds O \n30 - 50 pounds S \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds  \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 10% Sitting 80% Walking 2% Pulling  1% \nPushing    1% Lifting    2% Stooping    1% Kneeling    1% \nCrawling Climbing Reaching 1% Other   1% \n \n  \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu \n "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/IT%20Network%20Technician%200715026.pdf": {
      "title": "IT Network Technician",
      "description": "IT Network Technician.  Posted:  July 29, 2026 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:  IT NETWORK TECHNICIAN  DEPT:   ITC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $18.00 – 22.00 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 08/07/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nAssists in installation, maintenance, and repair of NMT communications infrastructure, local, and remote PBX \ntelephone equipment and services including MAC work orders. Maintains the daily operations, installation, \nmaintenance, testing, and repair of NMT fire alarm systems, intrusion detection systems, surveillance \nsystems, and any other low voltage systems. Maintains the daily operations, installation, maintenance, and \nrepair of NMT network infrastructure. This includes assisting/escorting contractors tasked with any of the \nabove work. Maintains sprinkler systems as well as assists/escorts contractors for these water based fire \nsystems. Uses cable locator to mark any underground communication lines, and keeps debris away from \nthese communication lines so that they are not to be damaged in any way. Maintains backup batteries and \ngenerators that help keep emergency services running during a power outage. After -hours standby/on-call \nduties will be required. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nInstall, troubleshoot, and repair Telecommunication, Fire Alarm, Security devices.  \n \nInstall, troubleshoot, and repair physical Telecommunication, Fire Alarm, Security lines on campus.  \n \nEscort outside contractors to various buildings for inspections and outside support. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nHigh School (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, composition and math. Read and comprehend \ninstructions, write information and complete simple forms. High School (or GED) level ability in spelli ng, \ngrammar, basic composition and math. Formal diploma/certificate/degree program of 6-18 months after high \nschool. Knowledge of water & clean agent based fire suppression systems. Experience with Intrusion Alarm \ninstallation and maintenance. Knowledge of  backup batteries and generators for these communications \nsystems. . Verifiable Industry experience required. \n \n \n \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nFormal diploma/certificate/degree program of 6- 18 months after high school  in Low \nVoltage/Telecommunications or Fire Alarm certifications.  Notifier Fire Alarm Systems, maintenance and \nprogramming desired. Experience installing low voltage cable desired and knowledge of low voltage cabling \npractices desired. Knowledge of National Electric Codes desired. Knowledge of National Fire Protection \nCodes desired. Knowledge of desktop computers required. Working knowledge of networking fundamentals \ndesired. Knowledge of NFPA 72, NEC, and NFPA  101 required. NM ES3J and ES7J or verifiable work \nexperience required by NM CID to obtain license. Successful applicant will apply for NM CID testing within 6 \nmonths of employment. \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds O \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding     25% Sitting    10% Walking 20% Pulling 8% \nPushing 5% Lifting 10% Stooping  2% Kneeling  5% \nCrawling   1% Climbing 10% Reaching  4% Other \n \n  \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/IT%20Support%20Technician%200610826.pdf": {
      "title": "IT Support Technician",
      "description": "IT Support Technician. Posted:  June 3, 2026 \n \n  \n \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: IT SUPPORT TECHNICIAN           DEPT: ITC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME       PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $18.00-$22.00 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe IT Support Analyst, Endpoint Support Services (ESS), as part of the Client Systems and Support \nDepartment (CSSD), provides support related to end -user computing, peripheral devices, printers, and \nbaseline software applications. This can include staging of new equipment for deployment across campus, \nbench testing, troubleshooting, and repair of computing equipment, forensic support and data recovery, as \nwell as onsite deployment of resources. The IT Support Analyst (ESS) supports end-user support via requests \nfrom the Service Desk, including end- user support, periodically back-filling the Service Desk, and providing \nsupport to the business office related to service billing. The IT Support Analyst (ESS) also supports the \ndevelopment of standard operating procedures and documentation of technical procedures for the CSSD. \nAdditionally, the position supports the Cybersecurity team via investigation and forensic data collection as \nneeded. Lastly, the IT Support Analyst (ESS) supports the 24x7 availability of critical university IT services via \nperiodic on-call rotation outside of business hours. The IT Support Analyst (ESS) is a user - and customer-\nfacing position, and plays an important part in the overall perception of the department. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nStage, troubleshoot, repair, install, and maintain computing devices, peripheral equipment, printers, and \nbaseline software.  \n \nService desk and end-user support  \n \nDocumentation, development of standard operating procedures, and billing support.  \n \nProvide investigation and forensic support to the Cybersecurity team Supervise and mentor student \ntechnicians \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nHigh School (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, composition and math.  Proficiency with computers, \ncomputing technology, and the setup and deployment of associated peripherals. Proficiency in the building, \ntroubleshooting, or repair of computing devices (e.g., desktops, laptops, etc.) Proficiency in the deployment, \nconfiguration, and use of Windows, Linux, and/or Macintosh operating systems.  Knowledge of networking, \nnetworking equipment, and network configuration in a business, educational, or enterprise environment.  \nProficient in working with office and productivity software (e.g., Microsoft Office, Google Workspace, etc.)  \nStrong motivation for customer service and end-user support. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nAssociates degree in Information Technology or closely related field. The degree requirement can be waived \nat the hiring supervisor's discretion based on at -least four years of relevant experience, plus one or more \nrelevant certifications. Experience with enterprise service desk software and billing systems. Proficiency in \nmanaging Microsoft Active Directory. Other certifications (e.g., Google IT Support, ITIL, M365 Fundamentals, \nCompTIA A+, etc.) are desired but not required. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds F \n50 - 100 pounds O \n100 + pounds S \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding  20% Sitting   15% Walking   20% Pulling  5% \nPushing    Lifting  10% Stooping    15% Kneeling     \nCrawling     Climbing    10% Reaching     5% Other \n  \n Apply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu  "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/NetwprkSpecII%20ITC%200916624.pdf": {
      "title": "Network Specialist II",
      "description": "Network Specialist II. Posted:  September 30, 2024 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:    NETWORK SPECIALIST II         DEPT: ITC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $ 48,672 - $61,570 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: October 9, 2024* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO \nAPPLY WITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \nInstalls and maintains campus data network equipment including core and edge devices. Also supports some \nrouter/layer 3 configuration.   \n \nInvolved in the installation and maintenance of application servers and/or services.  \n \nThis position is responsible for providing network support to administrative and academic users of with a \nvariety of physical and virtual PC software, hardware, security and network services. Also supports network \nprinters, scanners, IP telephones and other networked devices \n \nSupports the configuration, installation, monitoring, and operation of the wireless infrastructure. \n \nSupervise staff as well as student workers. \n \nOther duties as assigned. \n \nJOBS FUNCTIONS: \nCore and edge switch configuration, installation and management (Layer 2).  40% \nRouter and Firewall configuration, installation and management (Layer 3+).  10% \nSupervise/train support staff / Students.  5% \nInstall, configure, monitor, maintain, and backup servers, applications, and network devices. 25% \nEvaluate hardware and software for usability, security and viability.  5% \nMonitors network activity to determine and address capacity issues, security threats, and outages.   15% \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nHigh School (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, composition and math.  Experience with the \ninstallation and maintenance of TCP/IP network and data systems . Experience as a system administrator \nutilizing Windows 2016 (or above) operating systems . Experience as a system administrator utilizing \nUNIX/Linux. Able to obtain and maintain CJIS certification.  A valid and maintained New Mexico Driver's \nLicense.  \n \n \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nAssociate's degree or completion of program 18+  months after high school, IT, CS or similar; Appropriate \nwork experience may be substituted for some educational requirements. Any experience with IP Telephony, \nFirewalls, Traffic maintenance, Intrusion Detection and Prevention, Monitoring, and Diagnosing networks \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds O \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 20% Sitting 25% Walking 10% Pulling 5% \nPushing Lifting 10% Stooping 15% Kneeling \nCrawling Climbing 10% Reaching 5% Other \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Police%20Officer%200817526.pdf": {
      "title": "Police Officer",
      "description": "Police Officer. Posted: August 26, 2026 \n \nPOSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \nTITLE: POLICE OFFICER DEPT: CAMPUS POLICE \nREG 🗹🗹 TEMP ◻ FULL TIME 🗹🗹 PART TIME ◻ \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $25.00 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater. \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and ret irement which is largely paid \nby New Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH:  09/04/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING. APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThis is a critical position on campus. Fundamental purpose of the police officer is to provide assistance to \ncitizens in need while protecting students, staff, faculty, visitors and their property from harm. Officers are \nexpected to appropriately respond to emergencies, enforce state and local laws on a co-ed campus. \n \nJOBS FUNCTIONS: \n \nPatrol campus and assist citizens in need.  \n \nPatrol campus to deter crime, ensure safety and compliance with state and local laws.  \n \nOpen or Secure campus buildings EMRTC field lab sites as scheduled.  \n \nConduct thorough investigations of all alleged crimes.  \n \nQuickly, Completely, and Accurately complete incident reports and other documents.  \n \nParticipate in departmental training and activities  \n \nPerform other duties as assigned \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nHigh School (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, composition and math. Must be 18 years or older . \nRead, understand, and interpret laws, ordinances and operating procedures. Physical strength to chase \nand subdue fleeing persons. Ability to make split second decisions in stressful ,hostile, fluid situations that \naffects safety of self and others. Communicate well orally and in writing and be able to effectively use a \ncomputer. Valid NM driver's license. Possess a NMLEA Police Officer certification and be in good standing. \nSecurity clearance may be required. A pre-employment drug screen required upon hire. \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds F \n15 - 30 pounds O \n30 - 50 pounds S \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS: \nStanding 10% Sitting 43% Walking 30% Pulling 5% \nPushing 1% Lifting 5% Stooping 1% Kneeling 1% \nCrawling 1% Climbing 1% Reaching 1% Other 1% \n \n \n \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Research%20Administrator%201218025.pdf": {
      "title": "Research Administrator",
      "description": "Research Administrator. Posted:  December 11, 2025 \n \n  \n \nPOSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: RESEARCH ADMINISTRATOR (RA I)  DEPT:  SPONSORED PROJECT \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $60,000-70,000 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by New \nMexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 12/22/2025* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThis is an entry level position. Employee will learn to provide fiscal, administrative, and technical guidance and \nsupport to faculty and staff with ext ernal funding for research and other sponsored activities. Employee will \nlearn the basics of sponsored projects administration, to set up awards in the financial system, do all PI \nnotifications, approve all award expenditures, travel requests, graduate contracts, work authorizations, do \naward amendments and budget revisions, journal vouchers, payroll distributions and payroll journal vouchers. \nEmployee will learn invoicing, financial reporting, accounts receivable, and invoice reconciliations and will learn \nto coordinate with other offices to establish, track, and oversee cost sharing funds outside of the grants module. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nAward Acceptance: Learns to complete a comprehensive review of the award, identifying financial and \ninvoicing requirements. Learns the basics of the Banner grants module. Learns to establish new awards in the \nfinancial system, with understanding of the terms and conditions. Learns to identify pass -through agency \ninformation. Learns to set up new funding agencies in the financial system as required. Learns to maintain print \nand digital award documentation, uploading all documents to BDMS, recording awards in appropriate \nSponsored Projects log, and sending new award notices. Learns to coordinate receipt of awards with principal \ninvestigators, notifying PIs of all pertinent requirements.  \n \nAward Maintenance: Learns to prepare and/or assist PIs in placing proper written requests for award \nextensions, deviations, or modifications. Learns to monitor and/or initiate amendments to origi nal award \ninstruments. Learns to prepare budget revisions, journal vouchers, payroll accounting distributions, payroll \njournal vouchers as required, maintaining direct communication with PIs regarding all changes to awards. \nLearns to track the submission of invoicing and quarterly financial reporting. Learns to prepares close- out \ndocumentation and close out awards including review and analysis, invoice reconciliation, closing adjustments, \nfinal invoicing, and financial reporting as required. Learns to submit final reports required by agencies which \nmay include Subcontractor's Release, patent/invention reports, and/or property reports. Learns to ensure \nequipment disposition is obtained. Learns to assist in maintenance of sponsored research attribute database \ninvoice tracking system and accounts receivable. Learns to perform Banner financial system closeouts.  \n \nCompliance Administration: Learns to understand the requirements of each award, whether funding agency, \nfederal, state, or university regulations. Learns to advise PIs on administrative and financial matters and how to \nverify and enforce sponsor's terms and conditions. Learns to develop accounting applications, i.e. shadow \nsystems, as needed in order to satisfy various award requirements.  \n \nCash Management: Learns to evaluate awards for availability of funding, review requests to expend funds in \norder to ensure compliance with agencies' terms and conditions. Learns to monitor compliance with established \ninternal control systems. Learns to monitor billing on active accounts. Learns to review transactions to \ndetermine allocability and allowability of expenditures during the period of performance and after award \ntermination. Learns to approve expenditures in both Banner and Works. Learns to approve student contracts, \nwork authorizations, ePAFs, payroll distributions and other charges being posted to each award. Learns \nreasonable procedures to ensure timely collection of invoiced amounts. Learns to write off deficits and \nsurpluses and close out awards in the Banner system.  \n \nReporting and Audits: Learns to verify timeliness and accuracy of reports and data required to be delivered to \nthe government. May learn to perform internal audits of travel or other items as required. Assists with required \nsubrecipient monitoring and reporting. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor's Degree in Accounting, business administration, management, or other related fields. Minimum of 15 \nhours or equivalent experience of accounting plus one or more courses in business law. Computer experience \nin MS Excel, MS Word. Strong oral and written communication skills; high level of organizational skills, tact, \nperseverance, and judgment. Ability to use professional judgment and discretion in dealing with outside \nauditors and external agencies R Ability to participate in work assignments under the supervision of higher \nlevel employee, but also the ability to work independently on assignments of standard difficulty and with the \nability to schedule own workload and meet deadlines. Ability to use broad knowledge of practices and \nprocedures to handle complex assignments; ability to understand and interpret complex materials such as \nfederal regulations, legal interpretations, audit reports, and documentation of internal control systems. Ability to \nwork and adapt under a highly regulated and constantly changing environment. Willingness to research facts \nand overcome errors. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nKnowledge of 2 CFR 200, FARs, DFARS, DEARs, Banner grants module, and general knowledge of State of \nNM procurement regulations. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  O \n15 - 30 pounds S \n30 - 50 pounds  \n50 - 100 pounds  \n100 + pounds  \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 10% Sitting 65% Walking 5% Pulling 5% \nPushing  Lifting 10% Stooping  5% Kneeling   \nCrawling    Climbing  Reaching   Other \n \n Apply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Tax%20Compliance%20Analyst%200815926%20Redone.pdf": {
      "title": "Tax Compliance Analyst REVISED",
      "description": "Tax Compliance Analyst REVISED.  Posted:  September 29, 2026 \n \n REVISED  \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:  TAX COMPLIANCE ANALYST          DEPT:    BUSINESS OFFICE \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME     \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $70,720 – $80,000 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by New \nMexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 08/19/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nSupports the university’s tax compliance activities and serves as a resource for departments with tax-related \nquestions. Assists with federal, state, local, and international tax requirements, including tax reporting, \ninternational tax, employees working outside New Mexico, and other university tax matters. Researches tax \nrequirements, assists with required filings, maintains documentation, and works with departments across the \nuniversity to identify and resolve tax-related issues. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nInstitutional Tax Compliance & Reporting: Supports university tax compliance and reporting, including \ninformation reporting, sales and use tax, U BIT, tax -exempt bond compliance, and other institutional tax \nmatters. Reviews university activities for potential tax requirements, assists with required filings, maintains \nsupporting documentation, and works with departments and outside tax advisors as needed.  \n \nInternational Tax Compliance: Supports tax compliance for international employees, students, visitors, and \nvendors. Reviews documentation, assists with tax withholding and reporting requirements, including Forms \n1042 and 1042-S, and works with departments to resolve international tax questions.  \n \nOut-of-State & Employment Tax Compliance: Supports tax compliance for employees working outside New \nMexico. Researches applicable requirements and works with Human Resources and Payroll on registrations, \nwithholding, reporting, and other tax requirements related to employee work locations.  \n \nDepartment Support & Compliance: Researches tax laws and regulations, responds to tax-related questions, \nand assists with tax notices, audits, procedures, and internal controls. Works with departments to identify tax \nissues, develop guidance, and support compliance with university tax requirements. \n \n \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor's degree in Accounting, Business Administration, or a related field. Applicants with a degree other \nthan Accounting must have at least 15 credit hours in accounting. Five (5) years of professional experience in \ntax compliance, accounting, auditing, payroll, financial compliance, or a related field; or three (3) years of \nrelevant experience with a master's degree in Accounting, Taxation, Business Administration, Finance, or a \nrelated field. Knowledge of accounting principles and financial or tax reporting requirements. Ability to \nresearch, interpret, and apply laws, regulations, and guidance t o financial transactions and tax compliance \nmatters. Strong attention to detail and accuracy in reviewing financial information, documentation, and \nreporting. Ability to communicate technical information clearly to a variety of audiences. Strong analytical , \norganizational, and problem-solving skills. Proficiency with Microsoft Office, particularly Excel.  \n \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nMaster's degree in Accounting, Taxation, Business Administration, or a related field. Experience with tax \nreporting or compliance, such as information reporting, payroll tax, multi -state tax, or international tax. \nAdditional experience in tax, accounting, payroll, audit ing, or financial compliance. Experience in higher \neducation, government, or nonprofit environments.  Experience with Banner or similar financial systems. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  O \n15 - 30 pounds O \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding   10% Sitting   65% Walking   15% Pulling    \nPushing    Lifting   5% Stooping  5% Kneeling   \nCrawling    Climbing    Reaching     Other \n \n  \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Carpentry%20Lead%200302925.pdf": {
      "title": "Carpentry Lead",
      "description": "Carpentry Lead. Posted:  March 13, 2025 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: CARPENTRY LEAD   DEPT: FACILITIES MANAGEMENT \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $ 20.00 - $21.00  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 03/24/2025 CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \nPerform all aspects of carpentry work as required (framing, finish carpentry, masonry, etc.) with \ncompetence and expected to lead/instruct carpenters. Assist with estimates. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \nRough carpentry (wall framing and sheeting, tape, lathe) Finish carpentry (finish plaster, drywall finishes, \ncabinetry, etc.) Rough concrete/masonry (laying, cutting/tying rebar, form work) Concrete and masonry \nfinishing (floating/troweling, block/brick work) Lead/instruct carpenters. Heavy Equipment Operator \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nRead and comprehend instructions, write information and complete simple forms. High School (or GED) level \nability in spelling, grammar, composition and math. Thorough carpentry knowledge and related skills.  4+ \nyears relevant experience. Knowledge of relevant local building codes. Ability to read, analyze, and follow \nwritten drawings and plan sets. Heavy Equipment Operation \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds F \n100 + pounds O \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 60% Sitting 10% Walking 10% Pulling  10% \nPushing   10% Lifting 15% Stooping  10% Kneeling  15% \nCrawling  5% Climbing  5% Reaching 10% Other \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Controls%20Technician%200816326.pdf": {
      "title": "Controls Technician",
      "description": "Controls Technician. Posted:  August 19, 2026 \n \n  \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: CONTROLS TECHNICIAN        DEPT: FACILITIES MANAGEMENT \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME        PART TIME     \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $35.00 - $36.00 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 08/27/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nServes as the primary field technician responsible for installing, troubleshooting, and maintaining campus -\nwide building automation and control systems. This role will identify and resolve system inefficiencies to \nimplement energy-, cost-, and performance-based improvements. On Call 24-7-365. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nInspection, installation and repair of HVAC and refrigeration systems, including associated control systems \nincluding the Building Automation System  \n \nPerform routine and preventive maintenance to increase the longevity and efficiency of HVAC systems and \ncontrols  \n \nCalibrate and adjust thermostats, valves, and other controls for optimal performance  \n \nDiagnose and troubleshoot problems with HVAC equipment and controls  \n \nEnsure all installations and repairs meet safety and quality standards, adhering to relevant codes and \nregulations. Interpret blueprints and technical diagrams related to HVAC installations.  \n \nOther duties as assigned \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nHigh School (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, composition and math. Knowledge of HVAC Controls \nAbility to read and interpret blueprints and schematic diagrams. Excellent Problem-Solving Skills. Knowledge \nof Building Automation Systems \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \n2-5 years as an HVAC Controls Technician . Certificate in HVAC, Controls, or Building Maintenance \nTechnology \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds F \n50 - 100 pounds F \n100 + pounds O \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding  60% Sitting  10% Walking   20% Pulling    20% \nPushing    20%  Lifting   20% Stooping    10% Kneeling    10% \nCrawling    10% Climbing    10% Reaching     10%  Other \n  \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Painter%20Seasonal%200509226.pdf": {
      "title": "Painter (Seasonal)",
      "description": "Painter (Seasonal). Posted:  May 18, 2026 \n \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:    PAINTER  (SEASONAL)   DEPT: FACILITIES MANAGEMENT \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE  $15.00  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by New Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \nApplies paints, enamels, varnishes, stains or lacquers to protect and decorate interior and exterior surfaces, \ntrim, and fixtures or buildings, furniture, cabinets, and other structures.     \n  \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nPrepare and smooth surfaces using sandpaper, scrapers, steel wool, wire brushes, and paint removers; \nmask surfaces not to be painted. Apply putty, spackling compound, plaster, or other fillers to fill nail holes, \ncracks, joints, and surface defects. Chooses the proper type and color of paint and application for a specific \njob. Check the color to make sure it is the proper shade. Primes, paints, stains, or varnishes using a brush, \nspray gun, roller, or cloth. Erects scaffolding or sets up ladders to perform tasks above ground level. Cleans \nup upon completion of the job; cleans brushes, rollers, and spray guns; removes masking materials; removes \npaint from floors, windows, and other surfaces. Helps other trades as needed (other duties as assigned)    \n    \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nRead and comprehend instructions, write information and complete simple forms. High School (or GED) level \nability in spelling, grammar, composition and math. New Mexico Driver's License.  \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds F \n50 - 100 pounds F \n100 + pounds O \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 75% Sitting  5% Walking 5% Pulling 5% \nPushing   10% Lifting 20% Stooping 10% Kneeling 10% \nCrawling   5% Climbing 10% Reaching 20% Other \n  \n Apply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Plumber%20Journeyman%200510126.pdf": {
      "title": "Plumber Journeyman",
      "description": "Plumber Journeyman. Posted: May 18, 2026   \n \n  \n  POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:      PLUMBER JOURNEYMAN         DEPT:    FACILITIES MANAGEMENT        \n  \n \nREG   TEMP      FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $25.00          \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% \nwhichever is greater. All regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and \nretirement which is largely paid by New Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO \nAPPLY WITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nInstall, alter, or repair plumbing and mechanical systems. Includes Preventative Maintenance of \nmechanical systems (HVAC). \n \nJOB DUTIES: \n \nPreventative maintenance on mechanical systems. Plumbing Repairs. New construction -  plumbing \nand mechanical systems. Job ordering parts for projects and repairs. Emergency Standby Required. \nEmergency repairs after hours required / Other duties as assigned.   \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nMust be able to read and comprehend instructions, write information and complete simple forms.  High \nschool (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, basic composition and math required.  Complete \nknowledge of plumbing and mechanical system codes required.   Experience in the use of power and \nhand tools required.  Must have the ability to operate light duty trucks and equipment. Ability to operate \nsome heavy duty equipment. Ability to use computer. Must have a valid NM driver’s license.  \nJourneyman’s plumbing licenses required.  \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds F \n50 - 100 pounds F \n100 + pounds O \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 55% Sitting 15% Walking 15% Pulling 10% \nPushing 10% Lifting 10% Stooping 20% Kneeling 20% \nCrawling  10% Climbing   10% Reaching 10% Other \n  \n Apply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu  \n \n \n \n  "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Director%20OIC.pdf": {
      "title": "Director of Office of Innovation and Commercialization",
      "description": "Director of Office of Innovation and Commercialization.  \n \n \nNEW MEXICO TECH CAREER OPPORTUNITY \n \nDirector of Office of Innovation Commercialization \n \nReporting to the Vice President for Research | Socorro, New Mexico \n \nNew Mexico Tech (NMT) is seeking an innovative, dynamic, and collaborative Director of Innovation and \nCommercialization. In this pivotal leadership role, the Director will champion New Mexico Tech’s mission \nas an entrepreneurial university and serves as the primary catalyst connecting New Mexico Tech’s \ncommunity of faculty, student, and staff inventors with the regional and national entrepreneurial \necosystem. \n \nThe position reports directly to the Vice President for Research and collaborates closely with faculty, \nresearch center staff and directors, the New Mexico Tech University Research Park Corporation, and \nexternal partners. The Director will have a direct, visible impact on shaping the university's \nentrepreneurial legacy, working closely with top-tier researchers who are changing the world. \n \nKey Responsibilities include: \n• Strategic Leadership: Provide vision and oversight for New Mexico Techs intellectual property \n(IP) portfolio and commercialization strategies. \n• IP & Policy Management: Develop, refine, and implement policies related to patenting, \nintellectual property, and licensing opportunities. \n• IP commercialization: Lead the efforts and partner with the New Mexico Tech University \nResearch Park Corporation (NMTURPC) and its Board to actively seek out and secure \ncommercialization and licensing pathways for NMT intellectual property and patents. \n• Ecosystem Integration: Facilitate bridging the gap between New Mexico Tech inventors and \nexternal entrepreneurs, investors, and industry innovators across the State and Nation. \n• Campus Engagement: Work hand-in-hand with both academic and non-academic departments \nto develop and execute active plans for safeguarding and commercializing university IP. \nWhat We Are Looking For: \n• An individual with excellent communication skills who can communicate effectively with faculty, \nresearchers, students, New Mexico Tech executive staff at all levels, members of the Board of \nDirectors of the NMTURPC and with entrepreneurs, investors, and industry innovators across \nthe state and the nation. \n• Someone with experience (>5 years) in entrepreneurial environments and who is innovative, \nproactive, and energized by the unique ecosystem of a focused STEM research institution. \n• Proven experience and success in navigating the complexities of intellectual property, \ntechnology transfer, and university-to-market pipelines. \nMinimum Qualifications: \n\n• Masters degree required, PhD degree preferred. Graduate degree in STEM ﬁeld preferred, and \nacceptable in entrepreneurship, business administration, ﬁnance, or related discipline, but only \nwith demonstrated relevant experience in tech transfer and commercialization at a STEM \nuniversity or organization. \n• Demonstrated record of fiscal and operational management. \n \nPosition Type:  \nPermanent Full-Time. The primary location of the position is on-site in Socorro, NM. \n \nHow to Apply: \nApplication materials must be submitted online. Review of applications will begin immediately. Priority \ndate for full consideration of application is October 12, 2026. \n \nPlease include a cover letter, current resume or CV and a list of three professional references with \ncomplete contact information. \n \nFor questions or additional information please contact Vice President for Research Lique Coolen \nat lique.coolen@nmt.edu \n \nInterested candidates are invited to learn more and submit application materials through the New \nMexico Tech employment portal: https://www.nmt.edu/hr/employment.php \n \nNew Mexico Tech is an Equal Opportunity / Affirmative Action Employer. \n "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Director%20of%20Research%20Safety%20and%20Compliance%200715526%20REV.pdf": {
      "title": "Director of Research Safety and Compliance",
      "description": "Director of Research Safety and Compliance.  Posted:  July 31, 2026 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:  DIRECTOR OF RESEARCH SAFETY AND COMPLIANCE  \n \nDEPT:        OFFICE OF RESEARCH \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE Negotiable \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nNew Mexico Tech is searching for a new Director of Safety and Compliance. The Director provides strategic \nleadership, oversight, and dir ection for the university’s comprehensive research safety, and regulatory \ncompliance programs. This role is critical in fostering a culture of safety, accountability, and risk mitigation \nacross all academic, research, and operational units. The Director ensures the university complies with \nfederal, state, and local regulations while supporting a robust, high-impact research enterprise. The position \nreports directly to the Vice President for Research and collaborates closely with faculty, institutional \ncommittees, and external regulatory agencies. The Director oversees a team of staff and all research \ncompliance and safety programs and services at New Mexico Tech, including conflict of interest, export \ncontrol, research ethics and integrity, responsible conduct of research, hazardous materials, radiation safety, \nhuman and animal research protections. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nStrategic Leadership & Culture  \n• Develop, implement, and monitor a university -wide research safety and compliance strategic plan that \naligns with the institution's research and academic goals.  \n• Promote a proactive culture of safety, continuous improvement, and environmental stewardship across \ncampus.  \n• Draft, review, and update institutional policies and procedures regarding laboratory safety, hazardous \nmaterials, and regulatory compliance.  \n \nRegulatory Compliance & Risk Management  \n     • Ensure strict compliance with federal agencies such as OSHA, EPA, DOT, CDC, and NIH, as well as      \n     state and local bodies.  \n     • Oversee institutional committees (e.g., Radiation Safety Committee (RSC), Institutional Animal Care and \n    Use Committee (IACUC), Institutional Research Board (IRB), and Institutional Biosafety Committee (IBC)   \n    to ensure research protocols meet ethical and legal standards.  \n     • Establish and maintain a rigorous program of routine inspections, audits, and gap analyses for all            \n    research laboratories, clinics, and support facilities.  \n     • Serve as the primary point of contact for external regulatory inspections and investigations. Lead             \n     investigations into safety incidents or noncompliance, ensuring root-cause analysis and corrective actions \n     are implemented.  \n \nOperational Management  \n     • Oversee specialized safety programs, including:  \n            o Chemical Hygiene and Biosafety (including Select Agents/Toxins).  \n            o Radiation Safety and Laser Safety.  \n            o Hazardous Waste Management and Environmental Sustainability.  \n            o Human and Animal Research  \n     • Design and deploy comprehensive safety training programs for faculty, staff, researchers, and students. \n     Maintain accurate, auditable training records.  \n     • Collaborate with Campus Police and Emergency Management to develop and test response plans for     \n     hazardous material spills, lab accidents, and public health crises. \n \nPersonnel & Budgetary Management  \n      • Recruit, mentor, and evaluate a team of safety professionals, specialists, and technical staff.  \n      • Develop and manage the annual budget for the Safety and Compliance office, optimizing resource         \n      allocation for maximum efficacy.  \n \n5. Other duties as assigned by the Vice President of Research \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor's Degree in a STEM discipline. Master’s degree in Chemistry, Biology, Geology, Physics, \nEngineering, or a closely related sc ientific/regulatory discipline. A minimum of 5 years of progressive \nleadership experience in research laboratory oversight, specifically including chemical and/or radiation safety, \nand research safety risk management, preferably within a research university or a complex medical/scientific \nresearch facility.  Deep working knowledge of federal regulations governing academic research \nenvironments. Ability to exercise influence and drive compliance in an environment where authority is \ndecentralized (e.g., worki ng with independent Principal Investigators). Exceptional written and verbal \ncommunication skills, with the ability to translate complex regulations into actionable guidance during high-\nstress situations. Strong capacity to evaluate complex scientific prot ocols, identify latent risks, and propose \ncreative, compliant solutions that do not unnecessarily stifle research innovation \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nPhD degree in Chemistry, Biology, Geology, Physics, Engineering, or a closely related scientific/regulatory \ndiscipline. Certifications: Certified Chemical Safety Officer, Certified Safety Professional (CSP), Certified \nIndustrial Hygienist (CIH), or Certified Research Safety Professional (CRSP). Proven track record of building \ncollaborative relationships with diverse stakeholders, particularly tenured faculty, principal investigators, and \nexecutive leadership. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds   \n15 - 30 pounds  \n30 - 50 pounds  \n50 - 100 pounds  \n100 + pounds  \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 30% Sitting 35% Walking 25% Pulling  \nPushing  Lifting  10% Stooping   Kneeling   \nCrawling    Climbing  Reaching   Other \n \n  \nIn order to be considered, submit a letter of interest, CV and resume. For full consideration, applicati on \npackets must be submitted by August 31, 2026. \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/LMA%20Technician%20I%20Langmuir%200816026.pdf": {
      "title": "LMA Technician I",
      "description": "LMA Technician I.  Posted:  August 10, 2026 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:  LMA TECHNICIAN I          DEPT:    LANGMUIR LAB \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $15.00 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 08/19/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nMaintain Lightning Mapping Array. Verify proper operation. Keep software configurations on stations up to \ndate Keep real-time data flowing Advise on future upgrades. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nMonitor LMA station performance and adjust parameters/config files to maintain good performance.  \n \nMonitor Real Time LMA and quickly correct software failures  \n \nDirect field service of hardware  \n \nMaintain spare parts inventory and recommend replacement parts for repair  \n \nAssemble new stations from spare parts  \n \nOther duties as assigned. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nAssociate's degree or completion of progr am 18+ months after high school in Physics or Electrical \nEngineering. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor’s degree in Physics, Electrical Engineering, Atmospheric Science or General Engineering. 3+ years \noperating lightning mapping arrays. \n \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding   15% Sitting 70% Walking   15% Pulling   3% \nPushing   3% Lifting   1% Stooping  3% Kneeling  3% \nCrawling   3% Climbing   1% Reaching    6% Other \n \n  \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Postdoctoral%20Scholar%20NCKRI%200918826.pdf": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar - Located in Carlsbad, NM",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Scholar - Located in Carlsbad, NM. Posted:  September 11, 2026 \n \n LOCATED IN CARLSBAD, NM  \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:  POSTDOCTORAL SCHOLAR  \n  \nDEPT: NATIONAL CAVE AND KARST INSTITUTE \n \nREG LIMITED TERM  TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $ $60,000 - $65,000 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe postdoctoral scholar will complement NCKRI’s existing expertise and be responsible for data collection, \nanalysis, management, and for manuscript preparation for peer -reviewed publications. This is a \ndevelopmental, limited-term (typically 1 -2 years) car eer-path position that is restricted to graduates of \naccredited university Ph.D. or equivalent programs. Second year of funding is contingent on approval. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nManages a cave paleontology inventory project including developing a geospatial database, photogrammetry \nof resources, and prioritization method protective action.  \n \nPublish as lead and co-author, on cutting edge research in appropriate scientific journals, conferences, and \nother outlets as appropriate.  \n \nAssist as a subject matter expert in field of applicant’s background through:  \n? responding to public information requests,  \n? working with NCKRI outreach program to develop science communication, and  \n? assisting NCKRI Cave and Karst Management Specialist in addressing NPS needs in area of expertise  \n \nAttend conferences and other meetings to present research results, collaborate with colleagues, build \nrelationships with potential colleagues and students, and promote NCKRI and NMT  \n \nOther duties as assigned important to the function, success, and reputation of NMT/NCKRI \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nPh.D. or other doctorate level equivalent in Paleontology or related field. Ability to safely conduct fieldwork in \nremote desert settings.  Skill to plan and conduct credible C&K paleontological research in cost -effective, \nefficient, timely, and professional ways.  Excellent ability to communicate C&K science both orally and in \nwriting, using standard computer software. Ability to work collaboratively on an interdisciplinary team. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nKnowledge of cave paleontology in the Southwestern United States . Experience working in caves with \nvarying levels of access from accessible show caves to unexplored cave passages. Experience leading field \nteams to ensure safety of each team member, minimal resource impact, and successful data collection.  \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding  10% Sitting  50% Walking  30% Pulling \nPushing Lifting  Stooping    2% Kneeling    2% \nCrawling     2% Climbing    2% Reaching  Other   \n \n  \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Professional%20Intern%200918726.pdf": {
      "title": "Professional Intern - Located in Carlsbad, NM",
      "description": "Professional Intern - Located in Carlsbad, NM. Posted:  September 11, 2026 \n \n LOCATED IN CARLSBAD, NM  \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:  PROFESSIONAL INTERN  \n  \nDEPT: NATIONAL CAVE AND KARST INSTITUTE \n \nREG LIMITED TERM  TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $ $48,000 - $61,800 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nUnder direct supervision and mentorship of professional staff and/or faculty, the employee undertakes a \nrange of on- going activities and assigned projects of varying levels of professional complexity focused on \ncave resource management.  \n \nThis is a developmental, fixed-term (typically 1-2 years) career-path position that is restricted to graduates of \naccredited university Bachelors or Masters programs who have obtained their degrees no earlier than 5 years \nprior to the date of hire. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nUnder direction of professional staff, conducts/facilitates NPS -focused cave and karst technical assistance \nprojects in collaboration with natural resources specialists and subject matter experts. This includes projects \nthat involve risk management, inventory, management plans, mapping, data management, environmental \nimpacts, restoration, emerging threats, etc. Completion of projects includes providing timely technical reports \nand evaluations to parks and other collaborators.  \n \nParticipate in roundtable sessions and interdisciplinary working groups to identify broad management needs \nand develop consistent practices across NPS units. Participate in NPS cave and karst resource specialist \nquarterly meetings. Produce management aids for cave and karst managers.  \n \nAssists other NCKRI staff in C&K research, education, academic, or administrative projects with guidance \nfrom respective program leads.  \n \nRepresents NCKRI and NPS at professional meetings and facilitates cave and karst-related trainings.  \n \nOther responsibilities as assigned \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor's Degree in Natural Science, Geology, Biology, Physical Science or related field . Ability to obtain a \nNew Mexico Driver’s License. Excellent ability to communicate natural resource management needs, goals, \nscience, and policy, both orally and in w riting, using standard computer software.  Ability to work \ncollaboratively on an interdisciplinary team. Experience working in caves with varying levels of access from \naccessible show caves to unexplored cave passages.  Experience producing professional reports, papers, \ngraphics, etc. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nMaster's Degree in Natu ral Resources or related fields.  Cave & Karst resource knowledge.  Knowledge of \nlaws, policies, guidelines, and best practices relevant to cave and k arst management on public lands. \nExperience collaborating with research scientists on research that  is directly applicable to resource \nmanagement. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds O \n100 + pounds  \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 5% Sitting 70% Walking 5% Pulling \nPushing Lifting % Stooping    5% Kneeling    5% \nCrawling     5% Climbing    5% Reaching % Other  5% \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Sponsored%20Projects%20Administraor%20III%200919226.pdf": {
      "title": "Sponsored Projects Administrator III",
      "description": "Sponsored Projects Administrator III. Posted:  September 28, 2026 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: SPONSORED PROJECTS ADMINISTRATOR  III  \n \nDEPT: SPONSORED PROJECTS DEVELOPMENT \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $60,320 – 76,305 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by New \nMexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 10/07/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe Office of Sponsored Projects Development seeks a Sponsored Projects Administrator III, Contracts \nSpecialist. This position provides senior-level pre-award support focused on federal contract and agreement \ndevelopment, including proposals subject to FAR/DFARS requirements, Other Transaction Agreements \n(OTAs), and Rough Order of Magnitude (ROM) estimates. The position serves EMRTC, ICASA, Playas, and \nbroader institutional research units, and is the primary administrative liaison for complex federal contracting \nactivity prior to institutional signature. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nFederal Contract Proposal Development: Lead development of proposals under FAR/DFARS procurement \ncontracts, OTAs, and ROM estimates. Build task-based budgets structured by CLINs and deliverables; apply \nFAR Part 31 cost principles and 2 CFR 200 cost standards. Prepare representations and certifications (Reps \nand Certs) required under federal solicitations. Review solicitation requirements and identify institutional risk \nprior to submission. Coordinate with legal and the Authorized Organizational Representative on terms requiring \ninstitutional approval or signature authority. \n \nProposal Coordination and Submission: Coordinate intake and timeline management for proposals across \nfederal, state, private, and industry sponsors. Maintain internal and sponsor calendars; track actions in \nsubmission portals (Grants.gov Workspace, Research.gov, NIH ASSIST, DOE portals, Exostar). Complete SF-\n424 family and sponsor-specific forms; validate packages; coordinate final review and submission. Assemble \nall required non- technical components including Facilities and Other Resources, Data Management Plans, \nBiosketches, and institutional boilerplate.  \n \nCompliance and Regulatory Pre- Checks: Route internal approvals; verify PI eligibility; apply FAR/DFARS \nclause requirements and flow -down obligations; support export control screening; coordinate subrecipient \ndocumentation (commitment forms, scope, budget, F&A status, audit status). Maintain organizational profiles in \nfederal and defense consortium portals (e.g., Exostar). Identify compliance risks prior to submission.  \n \nPost-Submission and Awar d Transition Support: Compile Just -in-Time materials, protocol approvals, and \nsponsor clarifications. Coordinate with legal during early award actions including review of administrative \nexhibits, special terms, and representations and certifications. Hand off a complete, organized packet to \nSponsored Projects Administration.  \n \nFunding Discovery and PI Outreach: Maintain PI interest profiles; run recurring funding searches and deliver \ncurated opportunity alerts with key requirements, internal routing dates, and early risk flags. Coordinate limited-\nsubmission announcements and internal down-select timelines.  \n \nTraining, Templates, and Customer Service: Provide responsive PI support; own library of checklists, budget \nworkbooks, subrecipient packets, and routing guides with version control. Deliver focused trainings. Issue \nsponsor-update briefs and policy guidance as needed.  \n \nOther Duties as Assigned: Perform related duties as required and assigned by the Director, Sponsored \nProjects Development, including reasonable back-up coverage for teammates and time-sensitive tasks outside \nnormal hours during sponsor deadlines. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor’s degree in any field; business, public administration, finance, or related preferred. Relevant \nprofessional experience considered in lieu of specific degree field. 5+ years of direct experience in sponsored \nprojects administration at a research institution, with demonstrated expertise in federal contract procurement, \nproposal development, and pre- award compliance. Experience working directly with federal agencies and \ndefense sponsors. Demonstrated working knowledge of FARs, DFARS, DEARs, including clause \ninterpretation, flow-down requirements, Reps and Certs preparation, and cost proposal structure under FAR \nPart 31. Experience with federal procurement contracts (Cost-Plus, FFP) and OTAs. Working knowledge of 2 \nCFR 200 (Uniform Guidance), including allowability, allocability, and reasonableness standards; cost share \ndocumentation; subrecipient monitoring obligations at the pre- award stage; and applicable cost princ iples \nunder 2 CFR 200 Subpart E. Demonstrated knowledge of proposal budgeting, accounting principles, and \nexceptional attention to detail. Well-developed analytical and problemsolving skills. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nMaster’s degree in research administration, public administration, business, law, or related field preferred.  \nExperience with DOE/DoD/DHS proposals, subrecipient documentation, and export control basics. Knowledge \nof non- FDP subaward requirements including foreign entities and industry partners. Certified Research \nAdministrator (CRA) or Certified Federal Contracts Manager (CFCM) preferred. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  O \n15 - 30 pounds S \n30 - 50 pounds S \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \n \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 5% Sitting 90% Walking 5% Pulling  \nPushing  Lifting  Stooping   Kneeling   \nCrawling    Climbing  Reaching   Other \n \n  \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Subject%20Matter%20Expert%200612126.pdf": {
      "title": "Subject Matter Expert - SWIA/DoW Program",
      "description": "Subject Matter Expert - SWIA/DoW Program. Posted:  June 15, 2026 \n \n  \n                                                        \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:   SUBJECT MATTER EXPERT  - SWIA/DoW PROGRAM    \n \nDEPT:  SIA \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME      PART TIME       \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $125 - $150 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe Subject Matter Expert (SME) serves as a recognized authority supporting the Southwest Innovation \nAlliance (SWIA), a center of the New Mexico Institute of Mining and Technology (NMT) established in \npartnership with the United States Air Force Research Laboratory (AFRL). Under the SWIA’s Partnership \nIntermediary Agreement (PIA) authority, the SME provides advanced knowledge and strategic guidance on \nprojects supporting the Department of War (DoW), including the Department of the Air Force, joint \nwarfighting organizations, defense research laboratories, and associated industry and academic partners.  \n \nThis is a part -time appointment of up to 0.50 FTE. The SME applies deep expertise in military operations, \ndefense research and development, acquisition and requirements processes, and operational test and \nevaluation to advance programs that strengthen warfighter readiness, resilience, and mission \neffectiveness. The SME provides authoritative technical input to program design, evaluation, and \nexecution; reviews methodologies and results; r epresents the SWIA in engagements with senior DoW, \nindustry, and academic stakeholders; and mentors program staff. SMEs support competitive funding \nproposals and align project goals with programmatic priorities and sponsor requirements. By maintaining \nup-to-date expertise, they influence programmatic strategy, innovation, and long -term program objectives \nin direct support of the national defense mission. This position offers flexibility for remote or hybrid work, \nsubject to program requirements and the need for periodic on-site presence at Kirtland Air Force Base and \nother DoW locations. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nServes as a recognized subject matter expert providing authoritative technical, operational, and research-\nbased guidance on DoW programs critical to the SWIA mission; prov ides expert consultation and strategic \nadvice to the SWIA Executive Director, SWIA program managers, AFRL counterparts, and external \ncollaborators on complex defense, research, and technology challenges. ** *  \n \nContributes advanced expertise to the design, execution, evaluation, and refinement of high- impact DoW \nresearch, training, and technology transition initiatives; reviews and evaluates research methodologies, \nanalytical approaches, and technical results to ensure scientific rigor, operational relevance, and alignment \nwith DoW and sponsor requirements.  \n \nDevelops expert -level recommendations, technical reports, white papers, briefings, and scholarly or \nprofessional publications that inform programmatic decision-making and program advancement.  \n \nAdvises on emerging trends, technologies, methodologies, and policy or regulatory considerations relevant \nto defense research, operational test, and warfighter capabilities; reviews and provides expert feedback on \nresearch outputs, technical deliverables, and publications to enhance quality and mission impact.  \n \nSupports competitive research funding and partnership efforts by contributing expert technical input to \nproposal development, scope definition, methodologies, and feasibility analyses.  \n \nRepresents the SWIA as a recognized authority at professional conferences, DoW advisory panels, \nindustry forums, workshops, and collaborative engagements.  \n \nCollaborates with interdisciplinary teams to integrate specialized defense and operational expertise into \nbroader research, operational, or strategic initiatives under the SWIA PIA portfolio.  \n \nExercises independent judgment and discretion in addressing sensitive, complex, or high- risk technical, \noperational, or research issues, including those involving controlled or export-sensitive information.  \n \nMentors researchers, staff, and project teams by sharing advanced knowledge, best practices, and \nprofessional insights without formal supervisory responsibility.  \n \nPerforms other duties and r esponsibilities as assigned in support of the SWIA and other program/research \nobjectives, strategic initiatives, and specialized DoW programs. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nA bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution in engineering, a physical or natural science, operations \nresearch, national security studies, organizational or behavioral science, or a closely related field is \nrequired. An advanced degree (master’s or higher) in a relevant technical, operational, or professional field \nis strongly preferred.  Minimum of ten ( 10) years of progressively responsible experience in military \noperations, defense R&D, operational test and evaluation, or a related national security field. \nDemonstrated leadership of advanced programs involving joint, interagency, or multi -stakeholder \ncoordination, with a strong record of technical leadership, publications, or senior -level briefings. \nExperience working with or within the DoW, a military service, defense research laboratory, FFRDC, or \nequivalent national laboratory or defense industry setting. U.S. citizenship and ability to obtain/maintain a \nDoW security clearance and unescorted access to federal military installations, including Kirtland AFB. \nAbility to handle classified, controlled unclassified, and export -controlled information in accordance with \nDoW and NMT requirements, including all associated investigations and training.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nSenior operational experience in the Department of the Air Force or a joint command (e.g., rated officer, \noperational commander, or senior staff officer); familiarity with advanced training environments, warfighter \nreadiness and resilience programs, information warfare, electronic warfare, cyber, or nonkinetic \noperational domains.  Demonstrated experience in defense RDT&E, including requirements definition,  \nconcept development, and capability transition. Direct experience supporting AFRL, a major command \n(e.g., ACC or AETC), USSTRATCOM, or equivalent, including service as a DoW –industry– academia \nliaison or PIA support. Experience leading evaluations or operational assessments with defined measures  \nof effectiveness/performance.  Experience serving as a liaison among the Department of War, industry, \nand academia, including support to Partnership Intermediary Agreements or similar authorities.  \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds   \n15 - 30 pounds  \n30 - 50 pounds  \n50 - 100 pounds  \n100 + pounds  \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding   Sitting    Walking    Pulling   \nPushing    Lifting     Stooping     Kneeling     \nCrawling     Climbing    Reaching      Other \n  \n Apply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu  "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Buyer%20I%20EMRTC%200405626.pdf": {
      "title": "Buyer I",
      "description": "Buyer I. Posted:  April 2, 2026 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: BUYER I      DEPT: EMRTC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $16.00-$18.00  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 04/14/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nResponsible for the procurement of materials and supplies equipment and services for assigned area and \ncommodity within State, Federal and Institute Procurement Regulations. Support general accounting \noperations, assist with data gathering, and analysis. \n \nJOBS FUNCTIONS: \n \nReceives requests for the acquisition of materials, supplies, equipment and services; solicits price quotations \nand information for sealed bids; prepares quot ation abstracts; evaluates, negotiates and awards purchases \nwithin state Procurement Regulations, state Public Works Regulations and Federal Acquisition Regulations; \nsigns and releases purchase orders and related contract documents up to delegated authorit y. Establishes \nand maintains purchases, lease, rental and service contracts. Conducts periodic evaluation of purchases, \ncontract usage, price reviews and contractor performance.  \n  \nMonitor and expedite outstanding purchase orders to complete or close pur chase transactions. Receive \ninvoices, review for compliance with purchase order, resolve discrepancies, coordinate verification of \nsatisfactory receipt and obtain payment approval. Conduct periodic evaluations of outstanding and closed \npurchase orders. \n   \nMonitor, document and maintain all purchasing and credit card transaction records.   \n \nPerform other duties as assigned by Associate Director of Finance.   \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nHigh School (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, composition and math.  Skill in communicat ing in \nverbal and written form.  Skill in personal computer operations and computerized purchasing, accounts \npayable, inventory and receiving operations. Skill in evaluating, documenting and awarding purchase \ncontracts.  Skill in negotiations and conflict resolution.  A valid New Mexico driver’s license is required. \n \n \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nKnowledge of public purchasing practices and procedures. Knowledge of basic contract law as it relates to \npurchasing, inventory, shipping and receiving.  Knowledge of Federal Funded purchases under F ederal \nAcquisition Regulations. Knowledge of fund accounting practices and principles.  Skill in developing and \nwriting detailed specifications for sealed bids. Knowledge of Public Works construction contracting. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds O \n30 - 50 pounds  \n50 - 100 pounds  \n100 + pounds  \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 10% Sitting 80% Walking 5% Pulling \nPushing Lifting 5% Stooping Kneeling \nCrawling Climbing Reaching  Other \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Instrumentation%20Engineer%20I%200714526.pdf": {
      "title": "Instrumentation Engineer I",
      "description": "Instrumentation Engineer I.  Posted:  July 17, 2026 \n \n  \n \n  \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:  INSTRUMENTATION ENGINEER I                   DEPT:      EMRTC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $55,000 - $65,000 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 07/28/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nInstrumentation Engineer will work with a team of engineers and technicians to design, build, field, operate \nand maintain sensors, electronic instruments, data acquisition equipment, and control systems to support \nenergetic materials research and testing ate the EMRTC test site and other sites as required. This work will \nrequire circuit design, fabrication and programming of unique electronic packages for many types of \ninstruments. This position also requires working in the EMRTC field laboratory, running data acquisition \nsystems, cabling, installing connectors, troubleshooting electronic equipment, high speed digital camera set \nup, operation and maintenance. Working with acc elerometers, blast instrumentation and other diagnostic \nequipment \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nDesign and fabrication of electronic instrumentation  \n \nPrepare, transport, set up, troubleshoot and operate instrumentation systems to include the following: data \nacquisition systems, flash x-ray, doppler radar, and high-speed cameras.  \n \nOperate, troubleshoot, maintain, and repair electronic instrumentation equipment.  \n \nAssist with testing requirements using hand tools, power tools, and specialized equipment. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor's Degree in Electrical Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, Computer Sciences or Physics. Circuit \ndesign, electronics fabrication, programming. \n \n \n \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nSensor calibration and installation. Data acquisition experience. Explosives or energetics materials field \ntesting experience \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds O \n30 - 50 pounds  \n50 - 100 pounds  \n100 + pounds  \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 10% Sitting 20% Walking 25% Pulling  5% \nPushing     Lifting  15% Stooping    5% Kneeling    10% \nCrawling    Climbing   5% Reaching  5% Other \n \n Apply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Instrumentation%20Technician%20III%200612726.pdf": {
      "title": "Instrumentation Technician III",
      "description": "Instrumentation Technician III. Posted:  June 24, 2026 \n \n  \n \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: INSTRUMENTATION TECHNICIAN III      DEPT: EMRTC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME       PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $24.00 - $27.00 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 07/06/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe Instrumentation Technician III installs, operates, maintains, and repairs electronic instrumentation \nequipment. The Instrumentation Tech III works both in indoor and outdoor facilities to support EMRTC's \ntesting operations. This position requires all work to be performed safely and within operation guidelines. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nOrganizes and performs the preparation, transport, assembly, trouble shooting, and operation of \ninstrumentation data acquisition systems such as PXI, Synergy Systems, O -Scope, Flash Xay, Doppler \nRadar, and Highspeed Cameras in testing and testing scenarios at various test sites(working from test plans, \nwritten or oral instructions)  \n \nAssist senior personnel to prepare, transport, assemble, trouble shoot, and operate instrumentation data \nacquisition systems such as PXI, Synergy Systems, O -Scope, Flash Xay, Doppler Radar, and Highspeed \nCameras in testing and testing scenarios  \n \nOperate, Trouble shoot, maintenance, and repair electronic instrumentation equipment  \n \nUses mechanical hand tools, power tools, and specialized equipment including heavy equipment such as \nforklifts, man lifts, and others required for testing  \n \nAssist in training  \n \nAttends Safety meetings, communicates with all essential personnel, presents a positive and professional \nattitude, acknowledges and abides by New Mexico Tech safety policies and advocates others to follow policy \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nHigh School (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, composition and math. 8 Years current electronic \ninstrumentation experience or a minimum of an Associates Degree in Electronic/Computer Sciences or \nEngineering with 5 years work experience in similar field of work.  Proficient knowledge of personal computers \nand various software programs . Proficient in at least 6 of the following: Cameras, Digital Imaging, Data \nReduction, System Diagnostics, Lab Work, Field Lab Testing, Electronic Circuitry, High Speed Cameras, Data \nAcquisition, Radar Tracking, or Flash X-ray Imaging, LabView Program, VXI, or PXI R . Capable of reading \nand following test plans. Training Capabilities. A valid New Mexico driver's license is required. This position \nrequires a Department of Defense security clearance through Secret to be obtained within a reasonable time \nafter employment. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nExperience in field lab testing. Experience with LabView, MatLab, or other programming software. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds F \n50 - 100 pounds O \n100 + pounds S \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding  10% Sitting   20% Walking   20% Pulling  5% \nPushing   5% Lifting    15% Stooping    5% Kneeling    1% \nCrawling     Climbing   5% Reaching     5% Other \n  \n Apply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu  "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Research%20Engineer%20I%200918126.pdf": {
      "title": "Research Engineer I",
      "description": "Research Engineer I. Posted:  September 1, 2026 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: RESEARCH ENGINEER I  DEPT: EMRTC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $60,000 - $75,000  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 09/10/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe Research Engineer I supports the planning, execution, and analysis of tests under the guidance of a \nPrincipal Investigator (PI). They assist in advanced instrumentation and data collection techniques, \nincluding flash x -ray, Doppler radar, high- speed imaging, and Digital Image Correlation. Responsibilities \ninclude contributing to test plan development, proposal preparation, and post -test reporting. They also \nsupport t he design and fabrication of specialized test fixtures and articles, such as mounts, sabots, and \ntargets, and contribute to programming software needed to collect and analyze test data. This position \nemphasizes hands -on learning, technical growth, and innovation in test management and analysis while \nsupporting national defense objectives.  \n \nJOB DUTIES: \n \nParticipates in the planning, execution, and analysis of tests under the guidance of a Principal Investigator, \nincluding test range preparation, cleanup, coordination of field operations, and the application of \nengineering controls to ensure safety and efficiency.  \nAssists with and learns the processes for test plan and proposal development, as well as post -test \nreporting, ensuring alignment with customer specifications and project objectives.  \nSupports the design, fabrication, and assembly of specialized test fixtures and articles, such as mounts, \nsabots, and targets, while learning to incorporate engineering controls to meet unique testing \nrequirements.  \nParticipates in the use and learning of advanced data collection and analysis techniques with \ninstrumentation systems, including flash x -ray, Doppler radar, high- speed imaging, Digital Image \nCorrelation, and software tools; collects and analyzes test data, such as fragments and photos, to support \ntechnical reporting.  \nAssists in developing and learning to program software for data acquisition and analysis, ensuring \naccuracy and reliability in test results while adhering to established engineering controls. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nA Bachelor’s degree in Engineering is required, with preference given to candidates from disciplines such as \nMechanical, Electrical, Aerospace, or related engineering fields. A strong proficiency in mathematics and \napplied sciences is essential . Strong foundation in engineering principles, particularly in mechanical, \nelectrical, or aerospace disciplines. Familiarity with engineering controls and safety protocols in testing \nenvironments.  Excellent analytical, problem -solving, and com munication skills, with the ability to support \ntechnical reporting and documentation. Ability to obtain or possession of a valid New Mexico driver’s license. \nAbility to obtain and maintain a Department of Defense Secret security clearance within a reasonable time \nafter employment.  \n \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS:  \nExperience with programming and software development for data collection and analysis.  Ability to design, \nfabricate, and assemble specialized test fixtures and articles . Certifications or training in s afety protocols, \nsuch as OSHA or HAZMAT, are preferred but not required. Additional certifications in relevant software tools, \ndata acquisition systems, or engineering methods (e.g., LabVIEW, SolidWorks) are desired. \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds O \n100 + pounds  \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 20% Sitting 25% Walking 20% Pulling 5% \nPushing 5% Lifting 5% Stooping 5% Kneeling 3% \nCrawling   1% Climbing  5% Reaching 5% Other    1% \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/TRO%20II%20%200817626.pdf": {
      "title": "Test Range Operator II",
      "description": "Test Range Operator II. Posted: August 26, 2026 \n \nPOSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: TEST RANGE OPERATOR II DEPT: EMRTC \nREG  TEMP  FULL TIME  PART TIME  \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $16.50 - $17.50 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater. \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 09/04/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING. APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nConstructs, erects, cleans up and disposes of all materials/apparatuses necessary to ensure efficient and \nsafe testing procedures in the EMRTC Test Range Laboratory.  Performs all tasks in a safe and practical \nmanner. Constructs, erects, cleans up and disposes of all materials/apparatuses necessary to ensure \nefficient and safe testing procedures in EMRTC Test Range Laboratory. Performs all tasks in a safe and \npractical manner. Fabricates, prepares, erects, and cleans up targets/buildings/bunkers/structures on test \npads/ranges, utilizing metal plates, concrete, wood, soil, and celotex /Styrofoam. Targets range from \nstructures, buildings, airplane parts, Army tanks/armored personnel carriers, fuselages, tanks, and earthen \nberms; works from test plans, drawings, or oral instructions. Directs subordinate personnel performing task \nlisted above. Uses mechanical hand tools, grinders, measuring tools, hand and power tools, saws, slings and \nhooks, etc. Cuts various metals using oxygen/acetylene equipment. Welds in the horizontal , vertical, and \noverhead positions using proper machine settings and equipment. Operates heavy, medium, and light \nequipment such as tractors, loaders, forklifts, backhoes, motor graders, wreckers, dump trucks, flatbeds etc. \nAccording to certification and capabilities. Performs other functions i.e. assisting electrical, construction, \nphoto, instrumentation, mechanical, and ordnance personnel under the direction of qualified technicians or \nsupervisors. Attends safety meetings, acknowledges and abides by sick/annual leave policies, communicates \nwith all essential personnel, presents a positive attitude, acknowledges and abides by New Mexico \nTech/EMRTC safety policies/SOP’s/Security policies, etc. Provides on- the-job training, as required for test \nrange personnel and other personnel assisting in the execution of specific tasks. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nFabricates, prepares, erects and cleans up targets/buildings/bunkers/structures on test pads/ranges; utilizing \nmetal plates, concrete, wood, soil and celotex / styrofoam. Targets range from structures, buildings, airplane \nparts, army tanks/armored personnel carriers, fuselages, tanks and earthen berms; works from test plans, \ndrawings or oral instructions. Directs subordinate personnel performing tasks listed above. \n \nUses mechanical hand tools, grinders, measuring tools, hand and power tools, saws, slings, hooks, etc.; cuts \nvarious metals using oxygen/acetylene equipment; welds in the horizontal, vertical and overhead positions \nusing proper machine settings and equipment. \n \nOperates heavy, medium and light equipment such as tractors, loaders, forklifts, backhoes, motor graders, \nwreckers, dump trucks, flatbeds, etc. According to certification and capabilities. \n \nPerforms other functions i.e. assisting electrical, construction, photo, instrumentation, mechanical and \nordnance personnel under the direction of qualified technicians or supervisors. \n \nAttends safety meetings, acknowledges and abides by sick/annual leave policies, communicates with all \nessential personnel, presents a positive/professional attitude, acknowledges and abides by New Mexico \nTech/EMRTC safety policies / SOP’s/ Security policies, etc \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nHigh School (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, composition and math. Four (4) years’ experience in \ngeneral ordnance test range work; or a combination of years of relevant construction experience plus heavy \nequipment and welding experience; or three (3) years with Hiring Supervisors recommendation. Certified on \nall light equipment. At least two each of the following medium equipment - Backhoe, Skidsteer, Wrecker up to \nten tons, Cherry Picker, Frontend Loader. One each of the following heavy equipment -  Dozer, Scraper, \nMotor Grader, Crane, Tractor Trailer. Obtain within six months of hiring. Possess at least one of the following: \nClass III Crane License, Commercial Driver’s License with Bus endorsement. A valid New Mexico driver’s \nlicense is required. This position requires a Department of Defense security clearance through Secret to be \nobtained within reasonable time after employment. Commercial Driver’s License (if directed to obtain). \nWelding Certificate (if directed to obtain). \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nPossess at least one of the following: Welding certification, Employee possessors. Capable of welding, \ncutting all steel up to 1” horizontal, vertical and overhead. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds   F \n15 - 30 pounds   F \n30 - 50 pounds   F \n50 - 100 pounds F \n100 + pounds  \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS: \nStanding 25% Sitting 5% Walking 25% Pulling 5% \nPushing 5% Lifting 10% Stooping 10% Kneeling 5% \nCrawling Climbing 5% Reaching 5% Other \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/TRO%20Trainee%200817226R.pdf": {
      "title": "Test Ranger Operator Trainee",
      "description": "Test Ranger Operator Trainee. Posted:  August 21, 2026 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: TEST RANGE OPERATOR TRAINEE   DEPT: EMRTC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $15.00 - $17.00 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 09/01/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThis position is an entry -level position and will work out in the EMRTC Field Laboratory year round. Duties \ninclude assisting Test Range Operators in test preparation, setup, clean up, and may require physical labor \nfor many of the tasks. Trainee will be expected to work towards obtaining equipment on-the-job training and \neventual certification, Commercial Driver’s License, welding training and eventual certification, concrete and \nconstruction skills, and learning about explosives testing operations. This position will require attending all \nEMRTC safety trainings and learning about the EMRTC safety regulations. Performs all tasks in a safe and \npractical manner. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nAssist Test Range Operators to fabricate, prepare, erect and clean up targets/buildings/bunkers/structures on \ntest pads/ranges; utilizing metal plates, concrete, wood, soil and celotex/styrofoam. Targets range from \nstructures, buildings, airplane par ts, and army tanks/armored personnel carriers, fuselages, tanks and \nearthen berms; works from test plans, drawings or oral instructions.   \n \nBe in training to use mechanical hand tools, grinders, measuring tools, hand and power tools, saws, slings, \nhooks, etc.; cuts various metals using oxygen/acetylene equipment; welds in the horizontal, vertical and \noverhead positions using proper machine settings and equipment.  \n \nBe in training to operate heavy, medium and light equipment such as tractors, loaders, forkli fts, backhoes, \nmotorgraders, wreckers, dump trucks, flatbeds, etc. according to certification and capabilities.  \n \nPerforms other functions i.e. assisting electrical, construction, photo, instrumentation, mechanical and \nordnance personnel under the direction of qualified technicians or supervisors.  \n \nAttends safety meetings, acknowledges and abides by sick/annual leave policies, communicates with all \nessential personnel, presents a positive/professional attitude, acknowledges and abides by New Mexico \nTech/EMRTC safety policies/SOPs/Security policies, etc. \n \n \n \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nHigh School (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, composition and math. A valid New Mexico driver’s \nlicense is required. This position may require a Department of Defense security clearance through Secret to \nbe obtained within reasonable time after employment. \n \n \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds F \n50 - 100 pounds O \n100 + pounds  \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 25% Sitting 5% Walking 20% Pulling 5% \nPushing 5% Lifting 10% Stooping 10% Kneeling 5% \nCrawling Climbing 5% Reaching 5% Other \n  \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/MaintAssocTEMPPRTC2%200201025.pdf": {
      "title": "Maintenance Associate (3)",
      "description": "Maintenance Associate (3). Posted:  February 13, 2025 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: MAINTENANCE ASSOCIATE (3)   DEPT: PRTC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \n**Position located in Playas, NM \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $15.00-$16.50  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: February 25, 2025* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES \nWHO APPLY WITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \nPerform a wide range of tasks including but not limited to non- technical plumbing, electrical, heating/cooling \nand equipment use at a level not requiring license or certification. May be required to perform tasks in other \nfields such as light equipment operator, carpenter, painter and landscaper. May also be required to use light \nequipment such as backhoe, skid steer, self-propelled and riding lawn mowers. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \nMust be able to perform Non-technical maintenance heating and cooling systems.  10% \nMust be able to perform carpentry, painting and non-technical electrical, plumbing.  40% \nAbility to perform all aspects of landscaping including applicable equipment.  40% \nMay be required to assist site security officers.  10% \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nRead and comprehend instructions, write information and complete simple forms. High School (or GED) level \nability in spelling, grammar, composition and math.  Must record daily maintenance records. Ability to read, \nunderstand adhere to and enforce safety procedures and regulations including utilizing all personal protective \nequipment (PPE). Ability to use hand and power tools applicable to task or trade. Perform carpentry, drywall, \ncabinetry, painting and plumbing on residential, commercial or industrial buildings. Including new \nconstruction, maintenance and repairs. Must possess proficient knowledge in and use of paints, adhesives, \npaint sprayers, rollers, trials, textures and HVLP machines.  Valid New Mexico Driver’s License. A pre - \nemployment drug test required upon hire. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nSkilled in the use of computers, preferably in a PC, windows-based operating environment. Ability to perform \npreventative maintenance procedures. Must be able to install doors, window and window/wall type HVAC \nunits. \n \n \n \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 40% Sitting 5% Walking 40% Pulling \nPushing Lifting 15% Stooping 5% Kneeling 5% \nCrawling Climbing 10% Reaching 10% Other \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Mechanic%20I%20PRTC%200404225.pdf": {
      "title": "Mechanic I",
      "description": "Mechanic I. Posted:  April 8, 2025 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: MECHANIC I  DEPT: PRTC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME       \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $18.00-$24.00  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n*Regular Limited Term \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \nThe fundamental purpose of this position is to maintain, and repair light, medium, and heavy -duty \ngasoline/diesel vehicles and equipment in support of PRTC. The Part-Time Mechanic I performs field repairs \non a variety of vehicles and equipment and is required to work both indoors and outdoors depending on the \nequipment and location. The Part -time Mechanic I reports to the Maintenance supervisor for direction and \nscheduling repairs. This position requires formal  training at a trade or technical school in engine repair or \ndiesel engines. The applicant may be required to occasionally travel to t he main NM  Tech Campus in \nSocorro for training to perform repairs on fleet vehicles and equipment, or other sites as needed. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nRoutine Maintenance- perform oil changes, tire rotations, fluid checks, and filter replacement. Inspect vehicle \nsystems (brakes, exhaust, suspension, etc.) and report any concerns.  Repairs and Diagnostics- diagnose \nbasic mechanical issues with the fleet vehicles. conduct minor repairs to engines, transmissions and other \nkey vehicle systems. Conduct complex repairs when needed.  \nVehicle Inspections- Conduct pre and post-trip inspections to ensure vehicles are safe to operate. Ensure all \nrequired documentation for inspections and repairs is complete and filed. Parts and Inventory Management- \nMaintain inventory of necessary parts and supplies. Order parts as needed, ensuring t imely availability to \navoid vehicle downtime.  \nSafety and Cleanliness- Ensure a clean and safe work environment by adhering to safety protocols. Dispose \nof hazardous materials (e.g., used oils, filters) according to regulatory guidelines. \nRecord Keeping- Maintain accurate logs of vehicle maintenance and repairs. Track service history for all fleet \nvehicles to ensure timely follow-up. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nRead and comprehend instructions, write information and complete simple forms. High School (or GED) level \nability in spelling, grammar, composition and math.  Formal diploma/certificate/degree prog ram o f 6 -18 \nmonths after high school - General Engine Mechanics or Diesel Mechanics. Five (5) years mechanic \nexperience with heavy equipment maintenance and repairs . At least 5 year experience with fleet vehicle \nrepair and maintenance. \nThis position MAY require a Department of Defense security clearance through Secret to be obtained. A valid \nNew Mexico driver’s license \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nCommercial Driver’s License (CDL) with HazMat endorsement . Trade school or formal mechanical \napprenticeship.  \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds F \n50 - 100 pounds O \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 25% Sitting 10% Walking 25% Pulling 5% \nPushing 5% Lifting 10% Stooping 10% Kneeling 5% \nCrawling Climbing Reaching 5% Other \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Rehabilitation%20skilled%20laborer%200404325.pdf": {
      "title": "Rehabilitation Skilled Laborer",
      "description": "Rehabilitation Skilled Laborer. Posted:  April 8, 2025 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: REHABILITATION SKILLED LABORER   DEPT: PRTC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $23.00 - $30.00  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n**Located in Playas, NM. \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \nPerform a wide range of tasks including but not limited to non-technical plumbing, electrical, heating/cooling, \nand equipment use at a level not requiring license or certification. May be required to perform tasks in other \nfields, such as light equipment operator, carpenter, painter, and landscaper. May also be required to use \nequipment such as a backhoe, skid steer, light equipment self -propelled, and riding lawnmowers. Drywall, \nmasonry, carpentry, and light demolition. \n \nJOBS FUNCTIONS: \nMust be able to perform carpentry, painting, and non-technical electrical, plumbing, and drywall.   \nConcrete work.              \nEquipment operating.            \nAssist Groundskeepers.             \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nRead and comprehend instructions, write information and complete simple forms. Record daily maintenance \nrecords. Ability to read, understand adhere to and enforce safety procedures and regulations including \nutilizing all personal protective equipment (PPE). Ability to use hand and power tools applicable to task or \ntrade. Perform carpentry, drywall, cabinetry, painting, and plumbing on residential, commercial, or industrial \nbuildings.  Including new construction, maintenance and repairs. Valid New Mexico Driver’s License. A pre- \nemployment drug test required upon hire. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nAbility to perform preventative maintenance procedures. Must be able to install doors and windows. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds F \n50 - 100 pounds O \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 40% Sitting 10% Walking 10% Pulling \nPushing Lifting  30% Stooping Kneeling 10% \nCrawling Climbing Reaching  Other \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/SecurityOfficerII%20PRTC%201016125.pdf": {
      "title": "Security Officer II",
      "description": "Security Officer II. Posted:  November 3, 2025 \n \n   \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: SECURITY OFFICER II   DEPT: PRTC \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \n**Located in Playas, NM \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $18.15  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nPRTC has experienced growth over the past few years and is anticipating further growth over the next year. \nAs a remote facility with a long first responder response time, there is a need for PRTC to have a security \nteam that can ensure a secure environment and be able to respond to threats effectively. The physical \nsecurity requirements related to classified contracts are substantial for PRTC, including armed security \nresponse. As PTRC prepares for the physical security requirements necessitated by the DD -254 and ICD-\n705, there is a need for a security force 24 hours a day 7 days a week.  \n \nPerforms highly complex security enforcement work; work involves monitoring and / or pat rolling PRTC \ngrounds; receiving visitors and verifying reason for visit. Inspecting and safeguarding university buildings, \nproperties and equipment and directing traffic, as well as directing traffic.    \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nSecures premises and personnel by patrolling property ; monitoring surveillance equipment; inspecting \nbuilding, equipment and access points; permitting entry, responding to alarms and calls for service. Monitor, \nlogs and authorizes entrance and departures of employees, visitors and other persons to maintain security of \npremises. Prevents losses and damage by reporting irregularities; informing violators of policy and \nprocedures. May confront and/or apprehend persons/suspects in cases of unauthorized entry into university \nbuildings and work areas, malicious mischief, or other violations. Controls traffic by directing drivers or \nescorting vehicles as necessary. Ensures that lights are on or off as required and that doors and windows are \nlocked during routine patrols. Completes and submits reports by recording observations, information, \noccurrences, and surveillance activities; interviewing witnesses; obtaining signatures. Maintains \nenvironments by monitoring and setting building and equipment controls. Maintains PRTC's stability and \nreputation by complying with legal requirements. Ensures operation of equipment by completing preventive \nmaintenance requirements; following instructions; troubleshooting malfunctions; calling for repairs; evaluating \nnew equipment and techniques. \n \n \n \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nRead and comprehend instructions, write information and complete simple forms. High School (or GED) level \nability in spelling, grammar, composition and math.  US Citizen. Valid unrestricted NM Class D Driver's \nlicense. Must pass pre-employment criminal background check. Successful candidate must submit to post, \noffer, pre-employment physical examination and medical history check. Ability to obtain and maintain a DoD \nSecurity Clearance Ability to be firearms qualified and carry a firearm. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nNew Mexico state security certifications and level 1-3. New Mexico state law enforcement certification, or  1 \nyear experience in law enforcement and/or security field.  Firearms safety. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds O \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds  \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 30% Sitting 50% Walking 30% Pulling 20% \nPushing 5% Lifting 5% Stooping 5% Kneeling 5% \nCrawling 1% Climbing 5% Reaching 20% Other 5% \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Security%20Officer%20III%20PRTC%200303526.pdf": {
      "title": "Security Officer III",
      "description": "Security Officer III. Posted:  March 10, 2026 \n \n  \n Located in Playas, NM \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: SECURITY OFFICER III  DEPT: PRTC  \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $19.97 - $24.97  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe armed Security Officer III  position is necessary for PRTC’s security capabilities to meet the \nrequirements of our customers who utilize our facility.  \n \nTo provide for armed alarm response for certain secure on- site facilities in the event of an incident or \nalarm call. The armed security team’s coverage is to be spread throughout the week as we provide 24/7 \nsecurity coverage at PRTC.  \n \nPRTC has experienced high level of growth over the past few years and is anticipating further growth over \nthe next few years.  \n \nAs a remote facility with a long first responder response time, there is a need for PRTC to have a security \nteam that can ensure a secure environment and to be able to respond to threats effectively. The physical \nsecurity requirements related to classified contracts are substantial for PRTC, including an armed security \nresponse.  \n \nPRTC must provide for the physical security requirements necessitated by DD-254 and ICD-705. ** \n \nJOBS FUNCTIONS: \n \nSecures premises and personnel by patrolling property; monitoring surveillance equipment; inspecting \nbuildings; equipment and access points; permitting entry.  \n \nMonitors, logs and authorizes entrance and departures of employees, visitors and other persons to \nmaintain security of premises.  \n \nPrevents losses and damage by reporting irregularities; informing violators of policy and procedures.  \n \nMay confront and / or apprehend persons / suspects in cases of unauthorized entry into university \nbuildings and work areas, and malicious mischief or other violations  \n \nControls traffic by directing drivers. Ensures that lights are on or off as required and that doors / windows \nare closed and locked. \n \nCompletes and submits reports by recording observations, information, occurrences and surveillance \nactivities; interviewing witnesses and obtaining signatures.  \n \nProvides armed response to PRTC alarms, maintains annual firearms qualifications and proficiency.  \nMaintains PRTC's stability and reputation by complying with legal requirements  \n \nEnsures operation of equipment by completing preventive maintenance requirements; following \ninstructions; troubleshooting malfunctions; calling for repairs; evaluating new equipment and techniques.  \n \nPerforms miscellaneous job related duties as assigned. May require working holidays and weekends as \nnecessary. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nRead and comprehend instructions, write information and complete simple forms. High School (or GED) level \nability in spelling, grammar, composition and math.  US Citizenship. Valid unrestricted NM Class D Driver’s \nLicense. Must pass a pre- employment criminal background check.  Successful candidate must submit to \npost-offer, pre-employment physical examination and medical history check. Ability to obtain and maintain a \nDoD Security Clearance. Ability to be firearms qualified and carry a firearm. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nFormal diploma/certificate/degree program of  6-18 months after high school in Criminal Justice/Security. \nAssociate's degree or completion of program 18+ months after high school in Criminal Justice/Security. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 60% Sitting 20% Walking 20% Pulling 10% \nPushing 5% Lifting 10% Stooping 10% Kneeling 2% \nCrawling 2% Climbing 2% Reaching 10% Other  \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Admissions%20Advisor%201019526.pdf": {
      "title": "Admissions Advisor",
      "description": "Admissions Advisor. Posted:  October 5, 2026 \n \n  \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: ADMISSIONS ADVISOR DEPT: ADMISSIONS \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $42,000 -$48,000  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nRepresents New Mexico Tech to prospective students, their families, and school/community contacts. \nDevelops and implements recruiting initiatives that support application and enrollment goals and conducts a \nholistic review of first-year student applications. Plans and implements recruitment activities, including high \nschool visits, area receptions, and individual meetings. Develops recruitment communications via the Slate \nCRM and application system. Reports to the Director of Admissions or designated administrator. \n \nJOBS FUNCTIONS: \n \nRecruitment, Counseling, and Territory Management  \n \nAdvise and consult with prospective students and families on admission to NMT \n \nSupporting Programs, Events, and Visits  \n \nOther Duties as assigned \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nBachelor's Degree in Any area of study.  0-3 years’ experience in higher education admissions, recruitment, \nsales, customer service, marketing, public relations or related area. Demonstrate the highest professional \ndemeanor and ethical behavior while representing the institution. Possess a celebration of diversity, both in \nthe workplace and in student population Ability to work independently as well as establish and maintain \ncollegial relations with other College staff, faculty, and students. Ability to work collaboratively as a member \nof a close and integrated team of admission professionals. Excellent written and oral communication skills \nAbility to plan, organize and implement events, programs and projects related to the University’s enrollment \ngoals. A valid driver’s license, and the ability to participate in significant travel.   \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \nKnowledge of Slate or other  CRMs. Extensive knowledge of an university environment and undergraduate \nand graduate admission support. \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds F \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 40% Sitting 40% Walking 10% Pulling \nPushing    5% Lifting    5% Stooping Kneeling \nCrawling Climbing Reaching  Other \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Educational%20Assistant%200816226.pdf": {
      "title": "Educational Assistant",
      "description": "Educational Assistant. Posted:  August 19, 2026 \n \n  \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: EDUCATIONAL ASSISTANT        DEPT: CHILDREN’S CENTER \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME        PART TIME     \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $15.00 - $19.00 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 08/27/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe Educational Assistant supports the development and implementation of ongoing, developmentally \nappropriate activities that promote each child’s cognitive, spiritual, physical, language, social, and emotional \ngrowth. Working in collaboration with Early Childhood Educators, this role also assists with child observations, \nportfolio development, family engagement activities, parent conferences, and home visits, as applicable. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nClassroom Support, Supervision and Child Care  \n  \nMonitor daily attendance and maintain awareness of child counts and required ratios. Support the teaching \nteam in implementing lesson plans and daily activities. Supervise and engage with children, ensuring a safe, \nnurturing environment. Provide direct care for children ages 0–10, including bottle feeding, diaper changing, \nrocking, potty training, and other age-appropriate care as needed.  \n \nChild Observation, Documentation, and Recordkeeping  \n \nObserve children and communicate effectively with the teaching team regarding development and behavior. \nAssist in maintaining accurate records of each child’s progress and development. Support documentation \nefforts, including taking photos and recording observations to contribute to child portfolios and accreditation \nrequirements.  \n \nFamily Engagement and Communication  \n \nEstablish and maintain positive relationships with children and their families. Communicate daily activities and \nimportant information. Participate in family engagement activities, parent conferences, and home visits, as \napplicable.  \n \nClassroom Preparation, Organization, and Sanitation \n \nAssist with classroom setup, preparation of materials and activities, and general administrative tasks. Clean \nand sanitize equipment and toys daily, maintain an organized classroom, and ensure the room is prepared for \nthe next day.  \n \nProfessional Development and Compliance  \n \nMaintain required certifications (e.g., CPR/First Aid, fingerprint clearance, licensing requirements). Complete a \nminimum of 24 hours of early childhood training annually. If supporting a NM PreK Educator, complete 6 credit \nhours per semester toward an Associate’s degree in Early Childhood Education. Stay current with best \npractices and understand and follow emergency procedures and protocols. Perform other duties as assigned \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nHigh School (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, composition and math.  Aptitude for working with \nchildren in a child development setting. Ability to communicate with children in an age appropriate manner.  \nCPR and First Aid Certificates within 6 months of hire. Must have background check conducted prior to start. \nAdherence to the NAEYC Code of Professional Ethics. Adherence to the NAEYC Development Appropriate \nPractices. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nCoursework or active pursuit of an Associate’s Degree in Early Childhood Education or a related field. For \npositions funded through the NM PreK Grant, candidates must be actively enrolled and completing a minimum \nof 6 credit hours per semester toward an Associate’s Degree in Early Childhood Education. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds N \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding  15% Sitting  18% Walking   19% Pulling     \nPushing    5%  Lifting   18% Stooping    15% Kneeling     \nCrawling    10% Climbing  Reaching       Other \n  \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Fitness%20Assistant%201019626.pdf": {
      "title": "Fitness Assistant",
      "description": "Fitness Assistant. Posted:  October 5, 2026 \n \n  \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: FITNESS ASSISTANT DEPT: RECREATION AND WELLBEING \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $15.00 - $16.00  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 10/14/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe purpose of this position is to provide fitness classes to the faculty, staff, community members (who have \nmembership), and students. The fitness classes provide a first time experience without having to fully commit \nto a semester long course. This makes NMT Recreation more marketable providing a new experience that \nhas not been done at the university recently. The programs would be the same class for one month, two \ndays a week. Each instructor would host two classes a week. Each class would be an hour long of \ninstruction. Each instructor would have an hour of prep each week to prep for class time. To reach a variety \nof students, faculty, staff, and community members I would like to offer traditional and non-traditional classes. \nOne  fitness assistant would focus on non-traditional sports focusing on that skill for a duration of one month \ninstruction: pickleball, spikeball, disc golf, cornhole, and badminton. Second fitness assistant would focus on \nthe traditional fitness classes, kettlebell training, walking, stretching, barbell lifting, and circuit training. Third  \nfitness assistant potentially a personal trainer meets with one to three students/members interested in \nlearning how to workout efficiently, discuss nutrition, and build the student, faculty, staff, or members \nconfidence in the weight room. This opportunity creates: social experience, focus on improving physical help \nby moving  your body, provides inclusion in traditional training and nontraditional sports. \n \nJOBS FUNCTIONS: \n \nEach fitness assistant would create lessons plans of instruction for the skills and programming they are \nteaching. Each month the fitness assistants would focus on one nontraditional sport, type of training, and \nbenefits of being physically active  One fitness assistant would focus on non- traditional sports focusing on \nthat skill for a duration of one month instruction: pickleball, spikeball, disc golf, cornhole, and badminton. \nSecond fitness assistant would focus on the traditional fitness classes, kettlebell training, walking, stretching, \nbarbell lifting, and circuit training. Third fitness assistant potentially a personal trainer meets with one to three \nstudents/members interested in learning how to workout efficiently, discuss nutrition, and build the student or \nmembers confidence in the weight room. The lesson plans will be required to have modifications with \nchallenges or making the skill more adaptable to complete the task.  \n \nThe fitness assistant will have to setup equipment for the class daily as needed based on the lesson plans \nthey created. They need to provide hands on instruction. For example: show how to throw a bean bag or \nsquat based on  the lesson they are doing. The fitness assistant 3 would need to work with the student, \nmember faculty, or staff to create goals setting plan for their fitness, nutritional, or personal goals for the \nmonth.  The class will be offered two times a week for 50 minutes. They will need to show up 10-15 early to \nset up and stay 10-15 minutes after to clean up.  \n \nThe fitness assistant will be responsible for checking out  and returning the equipment they use for their class \nduring the day, make sure equipment is wiped down and clean after the use of it. Placed back where they got \nthe equipment from  \n \nThe fitness assistant will be responsible for creating a sign in sheet and turning this in to the director  \n \nThe fitness assistant will be responsible for working with the Recreation and Wellbeing Administrative \nAssistant, Assistant Director, or Director to make sure a location, time, and place for the fitness classes  \n  \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nHigh School (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, composition and math.  Experience in teaching or \ncoaching    \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nCertification in nutrition. Certification in personal training. Certification in goal setting. \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds 3 \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 40% Sitting 5% Walking 25% Pulling   5% \nPushing    5% Lifting    10% Stooping   5% Kneeling   5% \nCrawling   5% Climbing Reaching    5% Other \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Manager%20Housing%20Operations%20Revised%200918526.pdf": {
      "title": "Manager/Housing Operations REVISED",
      "description": "Manager/Housing Operations REVISED. Posted:  October 12, 2026 \n \n REVISED  \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: MANAGER/HOUSING OPERATIONS  DEPT: RESIDENTIAL LIFE \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $43,680-$50,230  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 09/21/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nManage the daily routines of the Residential Life Office. Serves as the office’s primary telephone and e-mail \nreceptionist and is available to answer general questions from students and parents. Publicizes important \nevents and information to the Tech community and on- campus housing residents. Supervise student \nworkers. Support the ADHO for all housing operations. Tasks include processing daily work orders for seven \nresidence halls, following up on work orders' completion, ongoing maintenance issues, and tracking and \nupdating room and meal plan reservations. Provide assessment support to the Assistant Director of \nResidential Education and provide budget support to the Director. This position will also be the point person \nfor website upkeep and managing social media sites and posts. \n \nJOBS FUNCTIONS: \n \nOffice Management  \n• Functions as a first point of contact for students and families for the Residential Life s taff  \n• Supervise and train Student Desk workers.  \n• Represents the Residential Life Office during Admissions and Orientation events as needed  \n• Direct visitors to appropriate locations and resources. Greets visitors, answers phones, and routes \nmessages.  \n• Establishes, maintains, and safeguards confidential files and information for the Residential Life Office. \nUpdates and maintains files for accuracy.  \n• Routes incoming mail. Distributes outgoing correspondence. Processes daily mail (incoming and \noutgoing) and signs for packages.  \n• Purchases office supplies and equipment. Inventories supplies and maintains warranties on office \nequipment.  \n• Attends training, runs errands, and gives tours as needed.  \n• Provides support to Residence Life Coordinators, Assistant Directors, and Directors as needed.  \n• Oversees organization and cleanliness of the main office, the Resident Assistant Workroom, and the \nstorage room. \n \nAssist the Assistant Director of Housing Operations  \n• Support the Assistant Director of Housing Operations with occupancy management, including housing \nand meal plans.  \n• Assist with student billing, housing deposits, and refunds as needed.  \n• Provide housing verification letters  \n• Issue keys, order replacements, and charge for lost keys.  \n• Monitors secure key usage by vendors and staff.  \n• Maintain the work order log for facilities, ITC, and laundry.  \n• Notifies residents of expected entry and/or other interruptions for maintenance.  \n• Coordinate with Auxiliary Services and the department’s maintenance liaison to promptly ensure work \norders not serviced by Facilities Maintenance (blinds, screens, some appliance repairs, etc.) are \nattended to.  \n• Work with the department facilities coordinator to maintain an inventory of purchase dates and service \nrequest dates/results for every residential unit's appliances, furniture, and flooring.  \n         \nAssist the Assistant Director of Residential Education  \n• Maintains the Residence Life website and publications, keeping Residential Life information current and \naccurate.  \n• Monitors and updates the department’s social media pages.  \n• Support the Assistant Director of Residential Education as requested. \n \nBudget support  \n• Assists the Director with budget oversight.  \n• Pays invoices using purchase orders and direct payments.  \n• Maintains a log of all invoices.  \n• Follows up on back orders and invoices.  \n• Makes sure proper purchasing procedures are followed.  \n• Monitors monthly accounting for correct billing \n \nOther duties as assigned \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nHS diploma or GED required, Additional coursework beyond diploma, classwork related to office \nmanagement or business preferred. 5-7 years of work experience.  2-3 years of office management \nexperience. Ability to communicate effectively with students, faculty, administrators, alumni, & the public.  \nCommitment to a philosophy of quality customer service. Demonstrated ability to work effectively in a multi-\ncultural environment. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nFormal diploma/certificate/degree program of 6 -18 months after high school in Office Management or \nBusiness. Website management. Budget monitoring. Working knowledge of the Internet and Social Media.  \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds O \n30 - 50 pounds S \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \n \n \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 20% Sitting 50% Walking 10% Pulling 5% \nPushing 5% Lifting 10% Stooping Kneeling \nCrawling Climbing Reaching  10% Other     10% \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Residence%20Life%20Coordinator%200817826.pdf": {
      "title": "Residence Life Coordinator",
      "description": "Residence Life Coordinator. Posted: August 26, 2026 \n \nPOSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: RESIDENCE LIFE COORDINATOR DEPT: RESIDENTIAL LIFE \nREG  TEMP  FULL TIME   PART TIME  \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $50,000 - $55,000 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater. \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING. APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nReporting to the Assistant Director for Residential Education (ADRE), the Residential Life Coordinator (RLC) \nis responsible for supervising 3-4 halls and a student staff of 12-14 Resident Assistants. The RLC will assist \nHRL staff in ongoing assessment practices, program review, and the dissemination/presentation of critical \nelements related to the mission and strategic plan of the university  and HRL. This position will work closely \nwith HRL staff, faculty, and other academic staff. This position is a live- on position. Living on campus is a \ncondition of employment, and for the convenience of the employer, the housing is on the premises of New \nMexico Tech. \n \nThe RLC will assist in the development, implementation, and assessment of a programming model that \nfocuses on student leadership development and academic success. The goal of the residential experience is \nto increase student retention while developing cultural competencies. During the summer, the RLC will work \nwith summer conferences, summer orientation, or summer housing.  This position works with the Office of \nHousing & Residential Life’s (HRL) Leadership Team to integrate academic initiatives into the student staff \ntraining programs. The RLCs will assist HRL staff in on-going assessment practices, program review, and the \ndissemination/presentation of critical elements related to the mission and strategic plan of the university and \nHRL. \n \nJOBS FUNCTIONS: \nResidential Hall Leadership & Supervision \n• Supports the Mission, Vision, and Values of HRL. \n• Oversee all aspects of programming and building operations for their assigned buildings. o This includes \nbut is not limited to maintaining building rosters, managing room change processes, managing work orders, \nmanaging the programming budget, responding to student concerns, and completing other duties as \nassigned. \n• Assists in setting strategic vision in areas of residential programming and assessment for their assigned \nresidential communities. \n• Assists in assessing and proposing relevant policies, procedures, and initiatives to create intentional \nlearning environments for residential students and the promotion of ongoing assessment. \n• Respond to emergent issues related to HRL student experience. \n• Supervises 6 to 14 RAs. \n• Conducts weekly staff and 1:1 meetings with RAs. \n• Completes weekly walkthroughs of the buildings in their assigned area. \n• Assists in hiring, training, supervision, and evaluation of Resident Assistants \n• Assist with the development of marketing and promotion of Residence Life initiatives \n• Support the development and evolution of HRL priorities, initiatives, and projects. \n \nResidential Programming \n• Works to facilitate HRL’s educational priorities, themes, and associated learning outcomes for the \nresidential experience as it relates to residential programming. \n• Plans educational events/programs with associated learning outcomes that align with the residential \nexperience. \n• Assist RAs in the planning and evaluation of educational/social programs. \n• Sponsor department-wide educational programs \n• Monitor, reconcile, forecast, and strategically plan for the spending of the programming budget of \napproximately $2,500. \n• Utilize best practices and data to assist affinity space staff in developing, enhancing, assessing, and \nevaluating their affinity space to ensure each affinity space is meeting its desired goals. \n• Assist with training staff (professional and student) assigned to work with affinity spaces within the \nresidential programming. \n• Provide guidance and resources for event planning related to affinity spaces and general residential \nprogramming. \n• Develop recognition and awards for academic initiatives \n \nAssessment \n• Assist in the assessment of the residential experience. \n• Assist in creating a programming assessment plan that includes the associated learning outcomes, data \ncollection plan, data analysis plan, reporting structure format, and “closing the loop” related to the cycle of \nassessment. \n• Assist in developing surveys and other quantitative data collection efforts; assist in developing focus groups, \ninterviews, and other qualitative data collection efforts. \n• Prepare report documents to showcase appropriate outcomes and data to various stakeholders. \n \nAcademic Intervention & Support \n• Support residential student learning in the residence halls \n• Identify collaborative opportunities with other academic support units such as Academic Advising, Career \nServices, OSL Learning Center, etc., to support students’ academic success. \n• Identify resources for programs and initiatives that support students’ academic success. \n• Assist in faculty/staff engagement and involvement in the residence halls. \nRecruitment, Selection, & Training \n• Assists in coordinating and leading student staff training. \n• Promotes and engages in professional development opportunities. \n• Actively engages in the recruitment and hiring of new employees. \n• Develops and fosters supportive working relationships, motivation, and staff engagement. \n• Communicate information to staff on an ongoing basis to influence staff engagement and to be a part of a \nlarger community. \n• Adjust leadership style as needed to achieve results. \n• Recognizes the value of and promotes a diverse workforce. Values and encourages diverse perspectives, \ncreativity, and teamwork. \n \nCollaborative Assignment (Responsible for one of the following): \n• Summer Conferences \nAssist with the housing placements/ building assignments for summer conferences. \nServe as the liaison between summer conferences staying on campus and HRL. \nWorking with the ADHO, oversee the turnover of rooms between conferences and academic year housing to \nensure they are repaired and ready for the next academic year, while also accommodating the needs of \nsummer conferences. \nAssist with the summer conference CA on call and crisis management training. \n• Summer Housing \nOversee all residents living in summer housing \nSupervise a summer RA staff of 4-6 RAs \nAssist with the planning of Fall and Winter Training \nOversee spring-to-summer moves and summer to fall moves \n• Academic Initiatives and Affinity Housing \nWork with OSL to oversee in-hall tutoring \nCreate ways to promote academic success in the halls \nAssist with the planning of Fall and Winter Training \nSupport specialized training and programming for affinity housing \n \nResidential Student Support \n• Provides behavioral intervention to students, including counseling, mediation, problem -solving, policy \nviolations, and referrals of individuals or groups of students regarding personal concerns, academic needs, or \npolicies. \n• Serves as a base-level conduct hearing officer within HRL. \n• Responds to and supports medical emergencies, safety and security emergencies, and students in crisis as \nneeded. \n• Reviews incident reports, follows up with students involved in documented incidents, determines various \nlevels of behavioral sanctions, and enters incident report data into computer records. \n• Interprets and counsels students on university policy violations and determines educational sanctions and \nor responsibilities for policy and behavioral infractions. \n• Serves in on-call professional staff duty rotation. \n \nOther Duties as Assigned \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nBachelor’s degree in any area of study with 1.5 years of experience as a Resident Assistant, RHA, other \nstudent leader, or other experience working directly with college students. Commitment to the philosophy of \nquality customer service. Knowledge of Microsoft Office, including Word, Excel, and Access. Experience that \ndemonstrates an understanding of academic initiatives, student development theory, and residential \nprogramming models. Exceptional oral communication and interpersonal skills. Demonstrated ability to work \neffectively in a multi-cultural environment. Excellent organizational, planning, analytical, and problem-solving \nskills Ability to obtain a New Mexico License and complete New Mexico Tech’s Defensive Driving Course. \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nMaster's Degree in Higher Education or related field of study and one or more years of Residential Life \nexperience beyond the RA position. Experience providing interpersonal counseling and behavioral \ninterventions. Experience in building management. Must be able to demonstrate the ability to organize work \nand work with little supervision. \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds F \n15 - 30 pounds O \n30 - 50 pounds S \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS: \nStanding 10% Sitting 50% Walking 20% Pulling 5% \nPushing 5% Lifting 5% Stooping Kneeling \nCrawling Climbing Reaching 5% Other \n \n \n \n \n \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Student%20Access%20Service%20Coordinator%200918426.pdf": {
      "title": "Student Access Services Coordinator",
      "description": "Student Access Services Coordinator.  Posted:  September 11, 2026 \n \n  \n \n 7 Day on Campus \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE:  STUDENT ACCESS SERVICE COORDINATOR   \n \nDEPT:   STUDENT ACCESS SERVICES \n \nREG      TEMP     FULL TIME     PART TIME      \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $36,000 - $41,000 \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by New \nMexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: 09/21/2026* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WHO APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe mission of the Office for Student Access Services (SAS) is to provide equal opportunities and access to \neducation, programs, and activities for students with disabilities by increasing campus accessibility and \nremoving barriers. Disability is an aspect of diversity integral to society supported by SAS and the Division of \nStudent Life and guided by the university's mis sion to foster a welcoming and inclusive community. We are \nlooking for a Coordinator to join our growing, fast -paced office in support of students with disabilities. A \ncommitment to inclusion, diversity, and equity, modeling inclusive behavior, and promoti ng a culture of \nbelonging are integral to the office's mission. A high standard of confidentiality when handling information is \nrequired. Reporting to the Director for Student Access Services (SAS) the Coordinator is the first point of \ncontact for students, faculty, and staff connecting with SAS. The Coordinator will maintain the day -to-day \noperations of the office and assist with training and supervising student workers. The Coordinator will be \nresponsible for the testing center, and student testing requests and procedures. Additionally, the coordinator \nwill serve as lead proctor on all Accuplacer exams. The Coordinator should have a working knowledge of \nADAA, Section 504 of the rehabilitation law, FERPA, and state and local laws and regulations regarding \ndisability and discrimination disabilities by increasing campus accessibility and removing barriers. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nProctoring: Proctoring -Process student testing requests and instructor testing forms. Organize the testing \ncalendar and coordinate with testing staff scheduling. Acquisition of note- takers,interpreters, testing \naccommodations, etc. Collaborate with instructors to ensure a smooth testing process. Evaluate, implement, \ncoordinate, and monitor approved accommodations for testing. Work with ITC f or cameras, monitoring \nequipment, and computer access. Ensure a safe and consistent testing environment adhering to all SAS \nTesting Center Policies  \n \nAdministration, Office Support - Create an environment of exceptional customer service. Assist in maintaining \nthe SAS website for accuracy, update materials, create new areas, and work with the NMT Communications \nDepartment for compliance with marketing standards. Collaborate with the NMT Communications Department \nfor WCAG compliance Train and supervise student workers, and note-takers. Working directly with students \nwith an array of disabilities. Maintain an updated office Standard Operating Procedures (SOP). Monitor and \nprocess email for three email accounts Maintain electronic and physical files. Other duties as assigned  \n \nEvent Assistance: Assist with SAS events-  Student Access Connections, Weeks of Welcome, Orientation, \nTabling Events, etc. Creating flyers, scheduling event spaces, and ordering supplies and food. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nAssociate's degree or completion of program 18+ months after high school. 2- 4 years progress experience \nand/or an Associate's degree required. Experience or educational background in post-secondary education, \nspecial education, psychology, business, or related field. Positive interpersonal skills to maintain a welcoming \nand supportive environment. Ability to communicate effectively with different university populations i.e. \nstudents, faculty, and staff Understanding of personally identifiable information/HIPAA and the confidentiality \nrequired in Student Access Services. Understanding of FERPA, HIPAA, and maintenance of student records. \nProficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, Adobe, and Google Forms. General understating of database systems. \nAbility to formulate solutions when unexpected problems arise with confidence and clarity. Ability to maintain \npositive relationships with other offices on campus. Must be able to work independently, meet deadlines in a \ntimely fashion, and maintain workload. Able to work nights and weekends as needed. The ability to be punctual \nand maintain consistent attendance in this position is critical.  \n  \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \n2 years of experience working with people of varying disabilities. Specific emphasis on Neurodiverse \npopulations such as Autism and ADHD.  A working knowledge of the American with Disabilities Act (ADA), \nSection 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, and the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA) and HIPAA \nPrivacy Laws \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  O \n15 - 30 pounds S \n30 - 50 pounds S \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds  \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding 15% Sitting 45% Walking 15% Pulling 1% \nPushing 5% Lifting 5% Stooping  1% Kneeling  1% \nCrawling   1% Climbing 1% Reaching  5% Other  5% \n \n  \n  \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    },
    "https://www.nmt.edu/hr/Substitute%20Early%20Childhood%20Teacher%200611326.pdf": {
      "title": "Substitute Early Education Teacher",
      "description": "Substitute Early Education Teacher. Posted:  June 8, 2026 \n \n  \n                                               \n POSITION ANNOUNCEMENT \n \nTITLE: SUBSTITUTE EARLY CHILDHOOD TEACHER DEPT: CHILDREN’S CENTER \n \nREG     TEMP     FULL TIME      PART TIME     \n \nSTARTING RATE or SALARY RANGE $15.00  - $19.00  \nEmployees being promoted to a higher classified position receive the minimum for the position or a pay rate adjustment of 8% whichever is greater.  \nAll regular positions also entitle the employee to several benefits including health, dental, vision, life insurance, and retirement which is largely paid by \nNew Mexico Tech for the employee and dependents. \n \nINTERNAL POSTING THROUGH: Concurrent* CONSIDERATION WILL BE GIVEN FIRST TO TEMPORARY AND REGULAR TECH EMPLOYEES WH O APPLY \nWITHIN THE 7 DAY INTERNAL POSTING.  APPLICATIONS RECEIVED AFTER THE 7 DAY POSTING MARGIN WILL BE CONSIDERED WITH OTHER OUTSIDE APPLICANTS. \n \n \nJOB SUMMARY: \n \nThe Substitute Educational Assistant supports classroom activities on a temporary, as-needed, and part-time \nbasis. This role assists with implementing developmentally appropriate activities that support each child’s \ncognitive, physical, language, social, emotional, and spiritual development.  \n \nWorking under the guidance of Early Childhood Educators and regular classroom staff, the Substitute \nEducational Assistant helps maintain continuity in the learning environment, supports supervision of children, \nand assists with classroom routines and daily activities as needed. The role may occasionally support basic \nobservations and communication with staff to ensure consistency in care and instruction during staff \nabsences. \n \nJOB FUNCTIONS: \n \nSupport classroom staff on a temporary, as -needed basis by assisting with daily attendance tracking and \nmaintaining awareness of child counts and required ratios. Follow direction from the teaching team to help \nimplement lesson plans and daily activities. Supervise and engage with children to help ensure a safe, \nnurturing environment. Provide direct care for children ages 0–10 as needed, including bottle feeding, diaper \nchanging, rocking, supporting potty training, and other age-appropriate care. Assist with maintaining smooth \nclassroom routines and continuity of care during staff absences.  \n \nAssist regular classroom staff with classroom setup, preparation of materials and activities, and basic \nadministrative tasks on an as -needed, temporary basis. Clean and sanitize equipment, toys, and frequently \nused surfaces in accordance with center health and safety procedures. Help maintain an organized, safe, \nand welcoming classroom environment. Ensure materials are put away and the classroom is left in a ready \ncondition for the next day or next scheduled use.  \n \nMaintain required certifications as applicable to assignment (e.g., CPR/First Aid, fingerprint clearance, \nlicensing requirements) and remain in good s tanding with all background and regulatory requirements. \nParticipate in required training and orientation for substitute staff, including ongoing professional \ndevelopment as assigned. Stay familiar with center policies, best practices in early childhood education, and \nemergency procedures and protocols. Perform other duties as assigned to support classroom and program \nneeds during temporary placement.  \n \nSupport positive, respectful relationships with children and their families while serving in a tempor ary \nsubstitute capacity. Communicate with classroom staff to share relevant daily information and support \nconsistent messaging to families regarding activities and routines. As needed and under the direction of \nregular classroom staff, may assist with family engagement activities and scheduled meetings to help ensure \ncontinuity of care and communication during staff absences. \n \nREQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nHigh School (or GED) level ability in spelling, grammar, composition and math. Aptitude of working with \nchildren in child development setting. Ability to communicate with children in an age appropriate manner. \nAdherence to the NA EYC Cod of Professional Ethics.  Adherence to the NAEYC Development Appropriate \nPractices. First Aid and CPR within 6 months of hire. Must have background check conducted prior to start \ndate. Must pass required NM Tech drug testing . Provide documentation of criminal records check within 3 \nmonths of hire.  \n \nDESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: \n \nPost-secondary education in any field is preferred but not required. \n \n \n \nLIFTING REQUIREMENTS: \n(f)requently, (o)ccasionally, or (s)eldom \n0 - 15 pounds  F \n15 - 30 pounds F \n30 - 50 pounds O \n50 - 100 pounds S \n100 + pounds S \n \n \nPHYSICAL DEMANDS:  \nStanding    15% Sitting 18% Walking 19% Pulling    \nPushing    5% Lifting  18% Stooping 15% Kneeling     \nCrawling   10% Climbing    Reaching     Other \n \n  \nApply to: nmtjobapps@npe.nmt.edu "
    }
  },
